B.bgH=s(["\\b(?:hijab|veil|niqab|burqa|dress|inherit\\w*|beat\\w*|hit\\w*|polygam\\w*|testimony|witness\\w*|lead\\w* prayer|imam)\\b"],t.s)
B.amx=new A.aN("women-equality",B.aZt,B.bgH,"A fair answer has two parts: what the texts give women, and where Muslims still argue.\n\nEqual in worth before Allah: \"Indeed, the Muslim men and Muslim women, the believing men and believing women, the obedient men and obedient women, the truthful men and truthful women\" (Qur'an 33:35), \"for them Allah has prepared forgiveness and a great reward\". And \"Whoever does righteousness, whether male or female, while he is a believer - We will surely cause him to live a good life\" (Qur'an 16:97). Both come from one soul (Qur'an 4:1), and no one is above another except in righteousness (Qur'an 49:13).\n\nRights the texts give a woman: a share of inheritance of her own (Qur'an 4:7), her own earnings and property (Qur'an 4:32), the mahr that is hers, and a say in her marriage: The Prophet said a matron should not be given in marriage without consulting her, and a virgin not without her permission, which is her silence (Sahih al-Bukhari 5136). The Prophet \ufdfa also said Abdullah ibn Amr reported the Prophet said the best of you are those who are best to their womenfolk (Sunan Ibn Majah 1978, sahih per Darussalam).\n\nWhere there are differences in the rules, which people notice: in most inheritance cases a son's share is twice a daughter's (Qur'an 4:11), and in a written debt contract two women may stand in for one male witness (Qur'an 2:282). Scholars explain these by the different financial duties the two carry; others, Muslim and not, feel they are unequal, and there is a living debate about how they apply today. Many customs that harm women in Muslim countries come from culture, not from the texts, and Islam does not excuse them.\n\nI cannot settle the debate, and this is not a ruling. If you tell me which rule troubles you, I will take it by itself and give the sources.\n\nSource: Qur'an 33:35, 16:97, 4:1, 49:13, 4:7, 4:32, 4:11, 2:282; Sahih al-Bukhari 5136; Sunan Ibn Majah 1978, sahih per Darussalam.\n[GO:/academy/womens]","women",!0,"")
B.aU4=s(["\\bwhy (?:does|do|is|are|should) (?:a |the )?(?:women|woman|daughters?|sisters?|females?|girls?)\\b.*\\b(?:inherit\\w*|half|share|less)\\b","\\b(?:women|woman|female|daughters?)\\b.*\\binherit\\w*\\b.*\\b(?:half|less|unfair|unequal|two|2)\\b","\\binherit\\w*\\b.*\\b(?:women|woman|female|daughters?)\\b.*\\b(?:half|less|unfair|unequal)\\b","\\bmale (?:gets|get|receives?|inherits?) (?:double|twice|two)\\b","\\bdouble (?:share|portion)\\b.*\\b(?:men|male|sons?|brothers?)\\b","\\bwhy do (?:men|sons|brothers) (?:get|inherit|receive) (?:more|double|twice)\\b","\\b4 ?11\\b"],t.s)
B.amh=new A.aN("inheritance-half",B.aU4,B.b,"The rule comes from the Qur'an: \"Allah instructs you concerning your children: for the male, what is equal to the share of two females\" (Qur'an 4:11). It sits alongside the verse that gave women a fixed, obligatory share of their own: \"For men is a share of what the parents and close relatives leave, and for women is a share of what the parents and close relatives leave, be it little or much - an obligatory share\" (Qur'an 4:7).\n\nIt is not \"women always get half\". The doubling applies to a son and a daughter inheriting from the same parent. In other cases they are equal or the woman receives as much: both parents get a sixth if the deceased left children (\"And for one's parents, to each one of them is a sixth of his estate if he left children\" (Qur'an 4:11)), and brothers and sisters through the mother share equally (Qur'an 4:12).\n\nThe reasons scholars give (this is explanation, not part of the verse): the man carries financial duties the woman does not. He must pay the mahr, and maintain his wife and children, and often other relatives, whatever his wife owns. A woman's inheritance, mahr and earnings stay hers, and she is not required to spend them on the household. So, they say, the two shares are not the same weight in practice.\n\nSome people still find it unfair, especially in families where women also support the household; Muslim scholars and writers discuss it, and I cannot settle it. The share itself is fixed by the Qur'an. For a real estate, a qualified scholar must work out each person's share, because it depends on who survives.\n\nSource: Qur'an 4:11, 4:7, 4:12.\n[GO:/academy/transactions]","inheritance",!0,"")
B.bkk=s(["\\b(?:shak\\w*|handshakes?|hand ?shakes?) (?:hands?)?\\b.*\\b(?:wom[ae]n|girls?|females?|ladies|lady|opposite|non ?mahram|ajnabi|ajnabiyyah|men|man|males?|boys?|coworkers?|colleagues?|boss|teacher|doctor|sister|brother in law)\\b","\\b(?:wom[ae]n|girls?|females?|ladies|lady|opposite (?:sex|gender)|non ?mahram|men|man|males?|boys?)\\b.*\\b(?:shak\\w* hands?|hand ?shakes?|handshakes?)\\b","\\b(?:hand ?shake|handshakes)\\b.*\\b(?:islam|haram|allowed|permissible|halal)\\b"],t.s)
B.alA=new A.aN("handshake-opposite-gender",B.bkk,B.b,'Scholars differ on this, so I will give you the views and the texts rather than one verdict.\n\nThe texts: Aisha reported that when women pledged themselves to the Prophet \ufdfa he took the pledge by words alone (Sahih Muslim 1866), and that his hand never touched the hand of a woman other than one he was permitted to (Sahih al-Bukhari 7214).\n\nViews:\n- Most scholars of the four schools say a man and a woman who are not close relatives (not mahram) should not shake hands, and that this is forbidden, with some allowing it with an old person where desire is not a concern.\n- Some contemporary scholars allow it when there is no desire and no other way to be polite, such as at work or in a society where refusing causes real hurt, while saying that not shaking is better.\n- Many Muslims politely avoid it either way. A smile, a hand on the chest and a warm greeting ("peace be upon you", or "good to meet you") is accepted by almost everyone, and you can say, kindly, "I do not shake hands, nothing personal".\n\nFor your own situation, follow your school or ask a scholar you trust. This is not a ruling.\n\nSource: Sahih Muslim 1866; Sahih al-Bukhari 7214.\n[GO:/huda]',"etiquette",!0,"")
