B.amm=new A.aN("wudu-mistakes",B.aZi,B.b5h,"Mistakes people often make in wudu, and the points they miss:\n- Missing the heels or part of the feet. The Prophet \ufdfa saw people hurrying and rubbing wet hands over their feet and called out: Abdullah ibn Amr reported that some people performed ablution hurriedly on a journey and their heels were dry, and the Prophet said woe to dry heels, because of Hell-fire, and told them to make their ablution thorough (Sahih Muslim 241).\n- Rushing, or wiping instead of washing. The face, the arms to the elbows and the feet must be washed, and the head wiped (Qur'an 5:6).\n- Skipping the elbows, the area under the chin, or between the fingers and toes, or not wiping the whole head (the schools differ on how much of the head must be wiped).\n- Anything on the skin that keeps water from reaching it, such as paint, thick nail polish or wax. Water must touch the skin.\n- Wasting water. The Prophet \ufdfa made wudu with about a mudd of water (Sahih al-Bukhari 201). Being careful with water is part of the sunnah.\n- Doubting again and again. If you are not sure whether you broke wudu, you stay in wudu until you are certain.\n\nFor the exact order of the steps, ask me \"how do I make wudu\" and I will list them.\n\nSource: Sahih Muslim 241; Qur'an 5:6; Sahih al-Bukhari 201.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-wudu]","wudu",!0,"")
B.aIA=s(["\\b(?:grade|graded|authentic|authenticity|sahih|saheeh|hasan|daif|weak|fabricated|real|true|genuine|source|reference|where is|number|reliable)\\b.*\\b(?:actions|deeds|works) (?:are|is) (?:only |judged )?(?:by|according to|with) (?:the )?intentions?\\b","\\b(?:actions|deeds|works) (?:are|is) (?:only |judged )?(?:by|according to|with) (?:the )?intentions?\\b.*\\b(?:grade|graded|authentic|authenticity|sahih|saheeh|hasan|daif|weak|fabricated|real|true|genuine|source|reference|number|reliable)\\b","\\binnama\\w*\\b.*\\b(?:grade|authentic|sahih|source|number)\\b"],t.s)
B.am_=new A.aN("intentions-hadith-grade",B.aIA,B.b,'"Actions are by intentions" is among the most firmly established of all hadith. It is reported by Umar ibn al-Khattab, and it is the very first hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari (Sahih al-Bukhari 1), and also in Sahih Muslim (Sahih Muslim 1907). Because both Sahih collections include it, scholars call it agreed upon, and neither gives it a separate grade: inclusion in the two Sahihs is the highest level of acceptance. It is also the first of the Forty Hadith of Imam an-Nawawi.\n\nIts meaning, in short: a deed is judged by what the person meant by it, so the same act can be worship or nothing depending on the intention. Look at the source card under this answer, or open the Hadith screen to see it with its number.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1; Sahih Muslim 1907.\n[GO:/hadith]',"hadith",!0,"")
B.aZ1=s(["\\b(?:pray|prayer|prayers|recite|read|say|salah)\\b.*\\bin english\\b","\\bin english\\b.*\\b(?:pray|prayer|prayers|recite|dua|salah|fatiha|quran)\\b","\\b(?:pray|prayer|recite|read|say)\\b.*\\b(?:in my own language|in my language|in urdu|in bengali|in bangla|in turkish|in any language|in another language|in a language other than arabic|in my mother tongue|in translation)\\b","\\bdo i (?:have to|need to|must|really need to) (?:pray|recite|say|read|learn).*\\barabic\\b","\\bcan i (?:pray|recite|say|read) .*\\b(?:own language|my language|translation)\\b","\\bpray without (?:knowing|learning|memori\\w+|speaking|understanding) arabic\\b","\\barabic (?:required|necessary|needed|mandatory|compulsory) (?:for|in) (?:the )?prayers?\\b","\\bi (?:dont|do not|cant|cannot) (?:speak|know|read) arabic\\b.*\\bpray\\b","\\bis it (?:okay|ok|allowed|permissible|valid) to pray in (?:english|another language|my language)\\b"],t.s)
B.anp=new A.aN("pray-in-english",B.aZ1,B.b,"Scholars differ, so here is the honest picture, then what to do now.\n\n- The Prophet \ufdfa taught the prayer in Arabic, and the Qur'an recited in it is Arabic. Most scholars (the Maliki, Shafi'i and Hanbali schools, and the two companions of Abu Hanifa) say the Qur'an and the words of the prayer must be said in Arabic, and that a person who really cannot yet say them says what they can and keeps learning.\n- A narrower opinion attributed to Abu Hanifa allowed the Fatihah in another language for someone who cannot recite Arabic; the later Hanafi scholars took the other view. Ask an imam of your school which applies to you.\n- Du'as in your own language: outside the prayer, you can ask Allah in any language, right after the salam. Inside the obligatory prayer, scholars differ on whether you may make a du'a in your own words.\n\nWhat to do now, as a new learner: do not wait to be perfect. If you cannot yet say al-Fatihah, the Prophet \ufdfa taught a man who could not memorise any of it to say \"Subhanallah, walhamdulillah, wa la ilaha illallah, wallahu akbar, wa la hawla wa la quwwata illa billah\" in its place while he learned (Sunan Abu Dawud 832, hasan per Darussalam). The New Muslim Path teaches the words step by step: Day 5 for the movements, Day 11 for the Fatihah and this fallback, and Day 13 for this very question.\n\nI am not giving a ruling; ask an imam about your own case.\n\nSource: Sunan Abu Dawud 832, hasan per Darussalam.\n[GO:/new-muslim]\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-prayer]","prayer words",!0,"")
B.b3l=s(["\\b(?:what if|if) i (?:forget|forgot|cant remember|cannot remember|dont remember|do not remember|dont know|do not know|mess up|make a mistake in|get stuck in|go blank in)\\b.*\\b(?:words?|prayers?|recit\\w+|fatiha|surah|dua|ayat|verses?|arabic|what to say)\\b","\\bi (?:forgot|forget|cant remember|dont remember|dont know|do not know|cannot remember) (?:the |some of the |what to say in |what to recite in )?(?:words|prayers?|recitation|fatiha|surah|what to say|what to recite)\\b","\\bi (?:dont|do not) (?:know|have) (?:any |the )?(?:words|surahs?|fatiha|quran|arabic) (?:to pray|for prayer|for the prayer|yet|memorised|memorized)\\b","\\bi cant (?:memorise|memorize|learn|remember) (?:the |any )?(?:fatiha|surahs?|quran|prayers?|words)\\b","\\bnot memori[sz]ed (?:the )?(?:fatiha|prayers?)\\b","\\bwhat (?:do|should) i (?:say|do) (?:if|when) i (?:cannot|cant|dont|do not) (?:know|remember|recite|say) (?:the )?(?:fatiha|words|surah|quran)\\b"],t.s)
B.b_2=s(["\\b(?:rakah|raka|sujud|ruku|forgot (?:the |an )?extra|tashahhud|sit)\\b"],t.s)
B.al1=new A.aN("forgot-prayer-words",B.b3l,B.b_2,'First: do not be afraid, and do not stop praying. Everyone who learns the prayer goes blank at times, and Allah sees the effort.\n\n- If you are in the prayer and cannot recall a word, say what you can, stay still, and carry on. A slip does not need you to start again.\n- If you have not yet learned al-Fatihah, the Prophet \ufdfa taught a man who could not memorise any of the Qur\'an to say "Subhanallah, walhamdulillah, wa la ilaha illallah, wallahu akbar, wa la hawla wa la quwwata illa billah" in its place until he learned it (Sunan Abu Dawud 832, hasan per Darussalam). The Path (Day 11) has these words in Arabic with the sound and the meaning.\n- Use the New Muslim Path to learn a little each day, and keep a card with the words beside you at first. Listen to a reciter for the sound.\n- If you forget a whole act (a bow, a prostration, a sitting) or are unsure how many rak\'ahs you prayed, there is a remedy: ask me "what is sujud as-sahw?".\n\nIf the question is about a prayer you already prayed and you are worried about whether it counts, a teacher can tell you; I cannot rule on it.\n\nSource: Sunan Abu Dawud 832, hasan per Darussalam.\n[GO:/new-muslim]\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-sujud-sahw]',"prayer words",!0,"")
B.b7N=s(["\\bwhat (?:do|should|can|to) (?:i |we |you )?(?:say|recite|read|do)\\b.*\\b(?:sit|sitting|sat|sit down|sit in|between the two|in the middle|after the second)\\b.*\\b(?:prayers?|rakah)\\b","\\bwhat (?:do|should|to) (?:i |we )?(?:say|recite|read)\\b.*\\b(?:when|while) (?:i )?(?:sit|sitting|sat)\\b","\\bwhat (?:to|do i|should i) (?:say|recite|read) (?:in|during) (?:the )?(?:last |second |final |first )?(?:sitting|sit)\\b","\\bwords (?:of|for) (?:the )?(?:sitting|sit)\\b","\\bwhat (?:comes|do i say|do we say) after (?:the )?(?:second |last )?(?:rakah|sujud|prostration)\\b"],t.s)
B.alX=new A.aN("sitting-in-prayer-words",B.b7N,B.b,"There are two sittings, with different words.\n\nSitting between the two prostrations (in every rak'ah): say \"Rabbighfir li\", meaning \"My Lord, forgive me\" (Sunan Abu Dawud 874, sahih per Darussalam).\n\nThe sitting at the end of the second rak'ah (and the last sitting of every prayer): say the tashahhud. The Prophet \ufdfa taught Ibn Mas'ud its words (Sahih al-Bukhari 831):\n\n\u0627\u0644\u062a\u0651\u064e\u062d\u0650\u064a\u0651\u064e\u0627\u062a\u064f \u0644\u0650\u0644\u0651\u064e\u0647\u0650\u060c \u0648\u064e\u0627\u0644\u0635\u0651\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0648\u064e\u0627\u062a\u064f \u0648\u064e\u0627\u0644\u0637\u0651\u064e\u064a\u0651\u0650\u0628\u064e\u0627\u062a\u064f\u060c \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0651\u064e\u0644\u0627\u064e\u0645\u064f \u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u064a\u0652\u0643\u064e \u0623\u064e\u064a\u0651\u064f\u0647\u064e\u0627 \u0627\u0644\u0646\u0651\u064e\u0628\u0650\u064a\u0651\u064f \u0648\u064e\u0631\u064e\u062d\u0652\u0645\u064e\u0629\u064f \u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u064e\u0647\u0650 \u0648\u064e\u0628\u064e\u0631\u064e\u0643\u064e\u0627\u062a\u064f\u0647\u064f\u060c \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0651\u064e\u0644\u0627\u064e\u0645\u064f \u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u064a\u0652\u0646\u064e\u0627 \u0648\u064e\u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0649 \u0639\u0650\u0628\u064e\u0627\u062f\u0650 \u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u064e\u0647\u0650 \u0627\u0644\u0635\u0651\u064e\u0627\u0644\u0650\u062d\u0650\u064a\u0646\u064e\u200f \u0623\u064e\u0634\u0652\u0647\u064e\u062f\u064f \u0623\u064e\u0646\u0652 \u0644\u0627\u064e \u0625\u0650\u0644\u064e\u0647\u064e \u0625\u0650\u0644\u0627\u0651\u064e \u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u064e\u0647\u064f\u060c \u0648\u064e\u0623\u064e\u0634\u0652\u0647\u064e\u062f\u064f \u0623\u064e\u0646\u0651\u064e \u0645\u064f\u062d\u064e\u0645\u0651\u064e\u062f\u064b\u0627 \u0639\u064e\u0628\u0652\u062f\u064f\u0647\u064f \u0648\u064e\u0631\u064e\u0633\u064f\u0648\u0644\u064f\u0647\u064f\n\nAt-tahiyyatu lillahi was-salawatu wat-tayyibat. As-salamu 'alayka ayyuhan-nabiyyu wa rahmatullahi wa barakatuh. As-salamu 'alayna wa 'ala 'ibadillahis-salihin. Ash-hadu an la ilaha illallah, wa ash-hadu anna Muhammadan 'abduhu wa rasuluh.\n\nMeaning: all greetings, prayers and good things are for Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and messenger.\n\nIn the last sitting, after this, you also send blessings on the Prophet \ufdfa and then give the salam (ask me \"salawat in prayer\" for those words). The New Muslim Path teaches all of this on Days 5, 13 and 21.\n\nSource: Sunan Abu Dawud 874, sahih per Darussalam; Sahih al-Bukhari 831.\n[GO:/new-muslim]\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-prayer]","tashahhud",!0,"")
B.bfY=s(["\\b(?:khula\\w*|khul|divorc\\w*|talaq|separat\\w*|leav\\w*|end(?:ing)?|escap\\w*|get out|break up|annul\\w*)\\b.*\\b(?:unsafe|not safe|abus\\w*|violen\\w*|hits|hit|hitting|beats|beating|beaten|hurts|hurting|harm\\w*|afraid|scared|frightened|danger\\w*|threat\\w*|controlling|coerc\\w*|fear)\\b","\\b(?:unsafe|not safe|abus\\w*|violen\\w*|hits|hit|hitting|beats|beating|beaten|hurts|hurting|harm\\w*|afraid|scared|frightened|danger\\w*|threat\\w*|controlling)\\b.*\\b(?:khula\\w*|khul|divorc\\w*|talaq|separat\\w*|leave (?:him|my husband|the marriage)|leaving (?:him|my husband|the marriage)|end the marriage)\\b","\\bcan (?:a |the )?(?:woman|wife|muslim woman|i) (?:ask for|get|seek|file for|apply for) (?:a )?(?:khula\\w*|khul|divorc\\w*)\\b.*\\b(?:safe|abus\\w*|hit|hits|beat|beats|harm\\w*|afraid|scared)\\b"],t.s)
B.bby=s(["\\b(?:my (?:son|daughter|friend|sister|mother|mom|aunt|cousin|neighbou?r|client|student)|her husband|his wife)\\b.*\\b(?:hits|beats|abus\\w*)\\b.*\\b(?:what should|how can i help|how do i help)\\b"],t.s)
B.amj=new A.aN("divorce-unsafe",B.bfY,B.bby,"Your safety comes first. If you are in danger now, go to a safe place and call your local emergency number. The help lines on this screen can talk it through with you in confidence.\n\nIslam does not ask you to stay in a marriage where you are being hurt or frightened. Allah commands that a wife be kept in kindness or released with kindness, and forbids keeping her to harm her: \"and do not keep them, intending harm, to transgress [against them]\" (Qur'an 2:231). \"And live with them in kindness\" (Qur'an 4:19). And if a couple separates, \"Allah will enrich each [of them] from His abundance\" (Qur'an 4:130).\n\nWhat a woman can do, in general terms:\n- Khul': she asks to be released, usually by returning the mahr or what is agreed, as in the case of Thabit ibn Qais's wife (Sahih al-Bukhari 5273).\n- A judge or a recognised Islamic court can end a marriage in which the wife is being harmed, even if the husband refuses. Many scholars, and the family courts of many Muslim countries, allow this. The schools differ on the conditions and on whether his agreement is needed for khul', so a scholar who knows your school and your country should confirm the steps for you.\n- The warning about a woman who asks for divorce \"without a strong reason\" (Thawban reported the Prophet said if any woman asks her husband for divorce without a strong reason, the odour of Paradise will be forbidden to her (Sunan Abu Dawud 2226, sahih per al-Albani)) is about asking without cause. Being harmed is a cause. It is not a warning to a woman in danger.\n- Your country's own law is separate and open to you: a protective order, a shelter, a lawyer or the police can help whether or not an imam is involved. You do not need anyone's permission to be safe.\n\nLeaving can be the most dangerous time, so plan it with a domestic violence service: where you will go, your papers and phone, and someone you trust who knows. Staying is not a failure, and neither is leaving. This is general information, not a ruling on your case, and it is waiting for scholar review. A scholar who understands abuse, or a Muslim women's service, can support you without pressing you to stay.\n\nSource: Qur'an 2:231, 4:19, 4:130; Sahih al-Bukhari 5273; Sunan Abu Dawud 2226, sahih per al-Albani.\n[GO:/support]","divorce",!0,"")
B.bf8=s(["\\b(?:does|do|is|are|did) (?:the )?(?:quran|islam|quran say|religion|god|allah)\\b.*\\b(?:allow\\w*|permit\\w*|say|says|let\\w*|right)\\b.*\\b(?:hit|hits|beat|beats|strike|hurt|hurts)\\b.*\\bme\\b","\\b(?:husband|he|man) (?:is |was )?(?:allowed|permitted|right) to (?:hit|beat|strike|hurt) me\\b","\\b(?:allowed|permitted) to (?:hit|beat|strike) me\\b","\\b4 ?:? ?34\\b.*\\b(?:hits me|hit me|beats me|beat me|hurts me|abuses me|my husband)\\b","\\b(?:hits me|hit me|beats me|beat me|hurts me|abuses me|my husband)\\b.*\\b4 ?:? ?34\\b"],t.s)
B.bnU=s(["\\b(?:what does 4 ?:? ?34 mean|explain 4 ?:? ?34|meaning of 4 ?:? ?34|tafsir)\\b"],t.s)
B.amR=new A.aN("abuse-verse-434",B.bf8,B.bnU,"No. Nothing in Islam gives anyone the right to hit you, frighten you or control you by force. If he is hurting you, your safety comes first: go to a safe place and call your local emergency number if you are in danger. The help lines on this screen are for you.\n\nI will not answer you with a verse in place of your safety, but you asked about 4:34, so here is what it does and does not say. \"But those [wives] from whom you fear arrogance - [first] advise them; [then if they persist], forsake them in bed; and [finally], strike them. But if they obey you [once more], seek no means against them\" (Qur'an 4:34) is about serious, persistent misconduct and comes last, after advice and separation in bed. Scholars set hard limits on it: not on the face, not to hurt, not to humiliate; many say at most a symbolic touch, and that leaving it is better; and many say that today it should not be used at all. Beating a wife to control her, in anger, or to leave marks is not what the verse allows. It is harm, and Islam forbids harm.\n\nThe Prophet \ufdfa never struck a woman (A'isha reported that the Prophet never beat anyone with his hand, neither a woman nor a servant, except when fighting in the cause of Allah (Sahih Muslim 2328)). He warned the men who beat their wives that they are not the best of you (Sunan Ibn Majah 1985, sahih per Darussalam). When a husband broke his wife's arm, the Prophet told him to let her go (Sunan an-Nasa'i 3497, sahih per al-Albani).\n\nYou can ask me about khul' and what a woman can do if she is not safe at home. This is general information, not a ruling on your case, and it is waiting for scholar review.\n\nSource: Qur'an 4:34; Sahih Muslim 2328; Sunan Ibn Majah 1985, sahih per Darussalam; Sunan an-Nasa'i 3497, sahih per al-Albani.\n[GO:/support]","abuse",!0,"")
B.bcX=s(["\\b(?:share|send|forward|export|email|e mail|print|post|screenshot|show)\\b.*\\b(?:this |our |the |my |whole |entire |full )?(?:chat|conversation|thread|messages?)\\b.*\\b(?:friend|someone|family|brother|sister|mum|mom|dad|wife|husband|teacher|imam|scholar|others?|people|whatsapp|facebook|instagram|online)\\b","\\bcan i (?:share|send|forward|export|email|print|save) (?:this|our|the|my|whole|entire|full)?\\s*(?:chat|conversation|thread)\\b","\\b(?:share|send|forward|export|email|print|save) (?:this |our |the |my )?(?:chat|conversation|thread)\\b","\\b(?:is|are) (?:this|our|the|my) (?:chat|conversation|thread|messages?) (?:private|saved|stored|shared|public|visible|recorded|kept)\\b","\\b(?:how|where) (?:do|can) i (?:share|send|export|save) (?:this |our |the |my )?(?:chat|conversation)\\b"],t.s)
B.ali=new A.aN("share-chat",B.bcX,B.b,'No. A whole chat cannot be shared from the app, and that is on purpose: what you ask me can be personal.\n\nMy side of the chat is not saved or sent anywhere. It stays on screen for this visit only, and it is gone when you leave, clear it, or close the app. So there is no chat to forward.\n\nWhat you can share:\n- Each of my replies has a "Copy this reply" button. It copies that one reply, with its sources, and a line saying it is from Ar-Rayaan. Your question and the rest of the conversation are not included.\n- Verses, hadith, du\'as and stories have their own Share button. It shows exactly what will be sent before anything is copied, and nothing is posted for you.\n- A screenshot is your own choice. Look at the screen first: it will show your own words.\n\nIf you want a friend or a teacher to see an answer, copy that reply, or ask them to put the same question to me.\n[GO:/settings]',"sharing",!0,"")
B.aQ3=s(["\\b(?:where|how)\\b.*\\b(?:change|turn off|turn on|stop|switch|set|control|manage|see|delete|clear|forget)\\b.*\\b(?:remember\\w*|memory|know about me|knows about me)\\b","\\b(?:what|how much)\\b.*\\b(?:do you|does hadi|does hadi) (?:remember|know)\\b.*\\b(?:about me|me)\\b","\\b(?:do you|does hadi) (?:remember|keep|store|save) (?:anything|things|what)\\b.*\\b(?:about me|i say|i tell)\\b"],t.s)
B.aly=new A.aN("hadi-memory-settings",B.aQ3,B.b,'Here is what I keep, and where you control it.\n\n- I do not save our chat. It stays on screen for this visit only and is gone when you leave, clear it, or close the app.\n- Settings has a switch called "Hadi remembers you". It is off by default. It only lets me greet you by the name on your account, and only when I answer through an AI service you set up yourself; the name goes to that service with your question. As a guest there is no name, so it changes nothing.\n- Settings, then Support & wellbeing, holds the check-in, private mode, quick exit and the safe word.\n- To forget everything at once, say "forget everything" in this chat, or use New conversation in the menu.\n[GO:/settings]',"privacy",!0,"")
B.aPJ=s(["\\b(?:five|5) pillars\\b","\\bpillars? of islam\\b","\\bwhat are the pillars\\b","\\bislamic pillars\\b","\\barkan e islam\\b","\\barkan ul islam\\b"],t.s)
B.aLI=s(["\\b(?:iman|imaan|faith|belief)\\b"],t.s)
B.amB=new A.aN("pillars-islam",B.aPJ,B.aLI,"Islam rests on five pillars. Ibn Umar reported the Prophet said Islam is based on five: to testify that none has the right to be worshipped but Allah and Muhammad is Allah's Messenger, to offer prayer, to pay zakat, to perform Hajj, and to fast in Ramadan (Sahih al-Bukhari 8).\n\n1. The testimony of faith (shahada): that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad \ufdfa is His messenger.\n2. Prayer (salah): the five daily prayers.\n3. Zakat: the obligatory charity given from wealth that reaches the nisab.\n4. Fasting (sawm) in the month of Ramadan.\n5. Hajj: the pilgrimage to Makkah, once in a lifetime for those who are able.\n\nThese are the acts of worship that hold the rest of the religion up. The six articles of belief (iman) are a separate list; ask me about those too.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 8.\n[GO:/academy/aqeedah]","pillars of islam",!0,"")
B.aRC=s(["\\bwhat is eid\\b","\\bwhat (?:is|are) (?:the )?(?:two )?eids?\\b","\\beid al ?fitr\\b","\\beid ul ?fitr\\b","\\beid al ?adha\\b","\\beid ul ?adha\\b","\\bbakra eid\\b","\\bmeethi eid\\b","\\bhow do muslims celebrate eid\\b","\\bwhen is eid\\b","\\bdifference between (?:the two )?eids?\\b","\\btwo eids\\b","^eid$","^eid mubarak$","\\bhow (?:to|do (?:i|we)) celebrate eid\\b"],t.s)
B.b9F=s(["\\b(?:prayer|salah|namaz|zakat|fitrah|fitra|story|dream|knife|son|ismail|ishmael|qurbani|udhiyah|dua|takbir|greeting)\\b"],t.s)
B.al5=new A.aN("eid-festivals",B.aRC,B.b9F,"Muslims have two festivals in the year, and both are days of worship, joy and giving.\n\n- Eid al-Fitr falls on the 1st of Shawwal and ends the month of fasting. Zakat al-fitr is given before the Eid prayer so the poor can celebrate as well.\n- Eid al-Adha falls on the 10th of Dhul Hijjah, after the day of Arafah in Hajj. It remembers Ibrahim's obedience, and those who can afford it offer a sacrifice. Allah says the sacrifice is about devotion, not the meat: \"Their meat will not reach Allah, nor will their blood, but what reaches Him is piety from you\" (Qur'an 22:37).\n\nBoth days begin with the Eid prayer in congregation. On Eid al-Adha the Prophet \ufdfa taught that the prayer comes first and the sacrifice after it (Sahih al-Bukhari 5545).\n\nFasting is not allowed on these two days: Umar said the Prophet forbade fasting on the day you break your fast and the day you eat the meat of your sacrifices (Sahih al-Bukhari 1990).\n\nIslam has no other festivals for the community, and the Prophet \ufdfa said there is an Eid for every people (Sahih al-Bukhari 952). Ask me how to pray the Eid prayer, about zakat al-fitr, or about Ibrahim's story.\n\nSource: Qur'an 22:37; Sahih al-Bukhari 5545, 1990, 952.\n[GO:/calendar]","eid",!0,"")
B.b0m=s(["\\blaylat(?:ul|ul-| al| el)? ?(?:al |el )?-?qadr\\b","\\blailat(?:ul)? ?(?:al )?-?qadr\\b","\\bnight of (?:power|decree|qadr|destiny)\\b","\\bshab e qadr\\b","\\bshab-e-qadr\\b"],t.s)
B.amq=new A.aN("laylat-qadr",B.b0m,B.b,"Laylat al-Qadr, the Night of Decree, is the night the Qur'an began to be revealed, and it falls in Ramadan. Allah says: \"Indeed, We sent the Qur'an down during the Night of Decree. And what can make you know what is the Night of Decree? The Night of Decree is better than a thousand months. The angels and the Spirit descend therein by permission of their Lord for every matter. Peace it is until the emergence of dawn\" (Qur'an 97:1-5).\n\nIts date is not fixed. Aisha reported the Prophet said to look for Laylat al-Qadr in the odd nights of the last ten days of Ramadan (Sahih al-Bukhari 2017). So people give those nights extra prayer, Qur'an and du'a.\n\nA'ishah asked what to say if she found the night, and the Prophet \ufdfa taught her a short du'a, \"Allahumma innaka afuwwun tuhibbul-afwa fa'fu anni\" (O Allah, You are Pardoning and You love to pardon, so pardon me). It is reported in Jami' at-Tirmidhi 3513, da'if per Darussalam. The graders differ on that report (some call it sahih, one calls it weak), and the du'a is a good one to say in any case.\n\nSource: Qur'an 97:1-5; Sahih al-Bukhari 2017; Jami' at-Tirmidhi 3513, da'if per Darussalam.\n[GO:/ramadan]","laylat al-qadr",!0,"")
B.b5o=s(["\\bsunn?ah (?:of|on|for) (?:friday|jumuah|jumah)\\b","\\bfriday (?:sunn?ah|etiquette|manners|etiquettes|rituals|habits|traditions)\\b","\\bwhat (?:to do|should i do|do muslims do) on friday\\b","\\bwhat is sunn?ah on friday\\b","\\bsunn?ahs? of jumuah\\b","\\bjumuah (?:sunn?ah|etiquette|manners)\\b","\\bwhy is friday (?:special|important|holy|blessed)\\b","\\bsignificance of friday\\b","\\bwhat to do before jumuah\\b"],t.s)
B.alD=new A.aN("friday-sunnah",B.b5o,B.b,"Friday is the best day of the week: Abu Hurayrah reported the Prophet said the best day on which the sun has risen is Friday, on which Adam was created (Sahih Muslim 854).\n\nWhat is taught for Friday:\n- Wash (ghusl) and go early. Abu Hurayrah reported the Prophet said whoever bathes on Friday like the bath of janabah and goes early to the prayer, it is as if he offered a camel in sacrifice, and the reward lessens for each later hour (Sahih al-Bukhari 881).\n- Clean your teeth with the siwak and wear perfume if you have it: Sahih al-Bukhari 880. (The scholars differ on whether the bath itself is obligatory or strongly recommended.)\n- Go to the prayer when the adhan is called and leave trade for it: \"when [the adhan] is called for the prayer on the day of Jumu'ah [Friday], then proceed to the remembrance of Allah and leave trade\" (Qur'an 62:9). Afterwards: \"when the prayer has been concluded, disperse within the land and seek from the bounty of Allah, and remember Allah often\" (Qur'an 62:10).\n- Listen to the khutbah quietly.\n\nAsk me about the Friday prayer itself, who must attend, and how many rak'ahs it has.\n\nSource: Sahih Muslim 854; Sahih al-Bukhari 881, 880; Qur'an 62:9, 62:10.\n[GO:/prayer]","friday",!0,"")
B.aQB=s(["\\btawh?[ei]+d\\b","\\btauh?[ei]+d\\b","\\bwhat is shirk\\b","\\bshirk\\b","\\bassociating (?:partners|others) with allah\\b","\\bwhat is the greatest sin\\b","\\bunforgivable sin\\b","\\bis shirk forgiven\\b","\\boneness of allah\\b"],t.s)
B.aTY=s(["\\b(?:game|names)\\b"],t.s)
B.alh=new A.aN("tawheed-shirk",B.aQB,B.aTY,"Tawheed is the belief that Allah is One in His being and in His right to be worshipped, with no partner. Allah says: \"He is Allah, [who is] One, Allah, the Eternal Refuge. He neither begets nor is born, Nor is there to Him any equivalent\" (Qur'an 112:1-4).\n\nShirk is the opposite: giving someone or something else a share in what belongs to Allah alone, such as worshipping, praying to or relying completely on another besides Him. The Qur'an treats it as the gravest wrong. Luqman told his son not to associate anything with Allah: \"Indeed, association [with him] is great injustice\" (Qur'an 31:13). And \"Indeed, Allah does not forgive association with Him, but He forgives what is less than that for whom He wills\" (Qur'an 4:48). That verse is about dying while still committing it; the door of repentance stays open for a living person.\n\nThe Prophet \ufdfa explained what Allah's right is: Mu'adh reported the Prophet said Allah's right on His servants is that they worship Him alone and associate nothing with Him, and the servants' right on Allah is that He does not punish the one who associates nothing with Him (Sahih al-Bukhari 2856).\n\nSource: Qur'an 112:1-4, 31:13, 4:48; Sahih al-Bukhari 2856.\n[GO:/academy/aqeedah]","tawheed and shirk",!0,"")
B.b71=s(["\\biddah\\b","\\biddat\\b","\\bidda\\b","\\bwaiting period\\b.*\\b(?:divorce|widow|death|husband|woman|women|wife)\\b","\\b(?:divorce|widow|death|husband|woman|women|wife)\\b.*\\bwaiting period\\b","\\bhow long must a (?:widow|divorced woman) wait\\b","\\b4 months and 10 days\\b","\\bfour months and ten days\\b"],t.s)
B.akP=new A.aN("iddah",B.b71,B.b,'Iddah is the waiting period a woman keeps after a divorce or the death of her husband. The Qur\'an sets it:\n- Divorce, for a woman who has her periods: "Divorced women remain in waiting for three periods" (Qur\'an 2:228).\n- Divorce, for one who no longer has periods or has not had them: "their period is three months" (Qur\'an 65:4).\n- Pregnancy, whether after a divorce or a death: "for those who are pregnant, their term is until they give birth" (Qur\'an 65:4).\n- A widow: "wait four months and ten [days]" (Qur\'an 2:234).\n- Divorce before the marriage was consummated: "divorce them before you have touched them, then there is not for you any waiting period to count concerning them" (Qur\'an 33:49).\n\nDuring the iddah of a divorce the Qur\'an tells the husband not to put her out of her home (Qur\'an 65:1). The details of what a widow may and may not do, and how to count the days in your case, differ in practice; ask a qualified scholar about your own situation.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:228, 65:4, 2:234, 33:49, 65:1.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-nikah]',"iddah",!0,"")
B.aVM=s(["\\bmahr\\b","\\bmehr\\b","\\bdowry\\b","\\bbridal gift\\b","\\bbride price\\b","\\bbride gift\\b","\\bhaq mehr\\b"],t.s)
B.an1=new A.aN("mahr",B.aVM,B.b,"Mahr is the gift a husband gives his wife on marriage. It is her right, and it belongs to her: \"And give the women [upon marriage] their [bridal] gifts graciously. But if they give up willingly to you anything of it, then take it in satisfaction and ease\" (Qur'an 4:4).\n\nIt does not have to be money. When a man had little, the Prophet \ufdfa asked him to look for something, even an iron ring, and then married them for what he knew of the Qur'an (Sahih al-Bukhari 5087). So it is agreed between the two of them, and kept simple.\n\nIf the husband divorces her before the marriage is consummated, half of the agreed mahr is due unless it is waived (Qur'an 2:237). A lavish mahr is not required, and the family or the husband should not make it a burden. The schools differ on whether there is a minimum amount, so ask a scholar of your school for the exact rule.\n\nSource: Qur'an 4:4, 2:237; Sahih al-Bukhari 5087.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-nikah]","mahr",!0,"")
B.beS=s(["\\bwhich madhh?ab\\b","\\bwhich (?:school|mazhab) (?:of (?:law|fiqh) )?(?:is|do|should|are)\\b","\\bbest madhh?ab\\b","\\bwhat madhh?ab\\b","\\bwhat school (?:of (?:law|thought) )?do you follow\\b","\\bwhich school (?:of (?:law|thought) )?do you follow\\b","\\bdo you follow (?:a|any) (?:madhh?ab|school)\\b","\\bshould i follow a madhh?ab\\b","\\bdo i have to follow a madhh?ab\\b"],t.s)
B.amO=new A.aN("madhhab-which",B.beS,B.b,"I do not follow one school and I do not rank them. A madhhab is a school of Islamic law, a way of working out rulings from the Qur'an and the Sunnah. The four well-known Sunni schools are Hanafi, Maliki, Shafi'i and Hanbali. They agree on belief and on the main acts of worship, and differ on some details (for example the number of rak'ahs of witr, or whether jewelry is subject to zakat). I say so when an answer depends on the school.\n\nWhich one is right for you is a personal choice that most people make by where they live and whom they learn from. The Qur'an teaches: \"ask the people of the message if you do not know\" (Qur'an 16:43). So pick a qualified scholar or teacher you trust, follow their guidance consistently and do not mix and match to find the easiest ruling. For a question about your own case, ask that scholar.\n\nSource: Qur'an 16:43.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-madhhab-overview]","madhhab",!0,"")
B.bdA=s(["\\b(?:paradise|jannah|heaven)\\b.*\\b(?:feet|foot)\\b.*\\b(?:mothers?|mom|mum)\\b","\\b(?:mothers?|mom|mum)\\b.*\\b(?:feet|foot)\\b.*\\b(?:paradise|jannah|heaven)\\b","\\bat the feet of (?:the |your )?(?:mothers?|mom|mum)\\b","\\bunder (?:the |your )?(?:mothers?|mom|mum)s? feet\\b"],t.s)
B.alB=new A.aN("mothers-feet",B.bdA,B.b,"The wording I can source is this one. A man named Jahimah came to the Prophet \ufdfa wanting to go out and fight, and asked his advice. Jahimah asked to go out and fight and the Prophet asked if he had a mother, and told him to stay with her, for Paradise is beneath her feet (Sunan an-Nasa'i 3104, hasan sahih per al-Albani). The scholars the app uses grade that report as sound. The shorter line \"Paradise lies under the feet of mothers\", as it is often quoted, is not the wording of that report, and I cannot verify it as a separate hadith, so it is safer to quote the an-Nasa'i wording.\n\nThe meaning is that serving and staying close to your mother is a path to Paradise. The Qur'an also puts kindness to parents right after the worship of Allah: \"And your Lord has decreed that you not worship except Him, and to parents, good treatment\" (Qur'an 17:23).\n\nSource: Sunan an-Nasa'i 3104, hasan sahih per al-Albani; Qur'an 17:23.\n[GO:/sources]","paradise and mothers",!0,"")
B.bbe=s(["\\bjanazah\\b","\\bfuneral\\b","\\bprayer (?:for|over) (?:the |a )?(?:dead|deceased)\\b","\\b(?:dead|deceased) (?:body|person|muslim)\\b.*\\bprayer\\b"],t.s)
B.b6D=s(["\\b(?:plans?|insurance|costs?|prices?|funds?|savings?|loans?|debts?|expenses?|prepaid|company|director|donat\\w*|collect\\w*|wash\\w*|bath\\w*|bury\\w*|burial|shroud\\w*|kafan|coffin|grave\\w*|cemeter\\w*|condolence\\w*|console\\w*|mourn\\w*|grief|grieving|cry\\w*|wail\\w*|hurt\\w*|attend\\w*|women|woman|ladies|late)\\b","\\b(?:story|stories|game|chess|app|screen|ludo)\\b"],t.s)
B.alE=new A.aN("janazah-prayer",B.bbe,B.b6D,"The funeral prayer (salat al-janazah) is prayed standing, with four takbirs. There is no ruku' (bowing) and no sujud (prostration), no adhan and no iqamah: the prayer is takbirs, recitation and du'a, then the salam. It is a communal duty (fard kifayah): when enough Muslims pray it, the rest are relieved.\n\nBefore you begin: be in wudu and face the qibla. The body lies in front of the imam and the people stand behind him in rows. The Prophet \ufdfa stood level with the middle of a woman's body when he prayed over her (Sahih al-Bukhari 1331); where the imam stands for a man differs between the schools.\n\nThe steps:\n1. Make the intention in your heart, then say the first takbir, \"Allahu Akbar\".\n2. After the first takbir: the Shafi'i and Hanbali schools recite al-Fatihah here, and Ibn Abbas did and said it is the Sunnah (Sahih al-Bukhari 1335). The Hanafi school recites the opening praise (thana') instead.\n3. Second takbir, then send blessings on the Prophet \ufdfa, as in the last sitting of every prayer.\n4. Third takbir, then make du'a for the deceased and for the Muslims. The Prophet's du'a, as 'Awf ibn Malik remembered it, begins: Allahumma-ghfir lahu, warhamhu, wa 'afihi, wa'fu 'anhu. In meaning: O Allah, forgive him, have mercy on him, give him peace and pardon him, honour his resting place and make it wide, wash him clean of his faults, give him a better home and family, admit him to Paradise and protect him from the trial of the grave and the Fire (Sahih Muslim 963).\n5. Fourth takbir. The Hanafi school then pauses briefly and gives the salam; the Shafi'i and Hanbali schools make a short du'a first.\n6. Give the salam to the right (many scholars also to the left).\n\nFour takbirs is what the Prophet \ufdfa said when he prayed over the Negus (Sahih al-Bukhari 1245). One companion reported that the Prophet once said five (Sahih Muslim 957); the schools follow four.\n\nRecitation after each takbir differs between the schools, so follow your own school or ask your imam. These school details are the standard teaching of the schools and are waiting for scholar review.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1331, 1335, 1245; Sahih Muslim 963, 957.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-janazah-prayer]","janazah",!0,"")
B.aZf=s(["\\b(?:wash\\w*|bath\\w*|shroud\\w*|kafan|coffin)\\b.*\\b(?:dead|deceased|body|corpse|janazah)\\b","\\b(?:dead|deceased|body|corpse|janazah)\\b.*\\b(?:wash\\w*|bath\\w*|shroud\\w*|kafan|coffin|bury\\w*|burial)\\b","\\bburial\\b","\\bhow (?:to|do (?:we|you|i)|are) (?:bury|buried)\\b","\\bghusl (?:of )?(?:the )?(?:mayyit|dead|deceased)\\b","\\bwhat happens (?:to|when) (?:a )?(?:muslim|someone|person) (?:dies|die)\\b"],t.s)
B.amH=new A.aN("janazah-care",B.aZf,B.b,"When a Muslim dies, the community owes them three things: washing, shrouding with a funeral prayer, and burial, done without delay.\n\nWashing: the body is washed with water, in an odd number of washes (three, five or more), starting with the right side and the parts of wudu, and camphor goes in the last wash (Sahih al-Bukhari 1253). Men wash men and women wash women; a husband and wife may wash each other according to the schools.\n\nShrouding: the Prophet himself was wrapped in three plain white cotton sheets, with no shirt and no turban (Sahih al-Bukhari 1264). Then the funeral prayer is prayed.\n\nBurial: the Prophet said to hurry with the funeral (Sahih al-Bukhari 1315). The body is laid in the grave on its right side facing the qibla.\n\nFor the details of washing, shrouding and burying, the Huda guides in this app go step by step. Where your school differs, ask your imam.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1253, 1264, 1315.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-janazah-wash]\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-janazah-burial]","janazah",!0,"")
B.bnL=s(["\\bwitr\\b"],t.s)
B.bbr=s(["\\b(?:app|game|screen|guide)\\b"],t.s)
B.al0=new A.aN("witr",B.bnL,B.bbr,"Witr is the odd-numbered prayer of the night, prayed after Isha and before Fajr. The Prophet \ufdfa told us to make it the last prayer of the night (Sahih al-Bukhari 998).\n\nHow many rak'ahs depends on the school:\n- Hanafi: witr is wajib (a strongly obligatory prayer, just below fard). It is three rak'ahs with one salam, with the qunut du'a said in the third rak'ah before the ruku'.\n- Shafi'i, Maliki and Hanbali: witr is a strongly recommended sunnah (sunnah mu'akkadah). It can be one rak'ah or three, five and so on up to eleven. Most people pray two rak'ahs, give the salam, then one rak'ah.\n\nThe Prophet \ufdfa taught the night prayer as pairs of rak'ahs, with one rak'ah at the end if you fear that dawn is near, to make the total odd (Sahih Muslim 749).\n\nIf you miss witr, the Hanafi school says to make it up; the others say it is a missed sunnah. Ask a scholar of your school for the details of qunut.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 998; Sahih Muslim 749.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-witr]","witr",!0,"")
B.bmh=s(["\\btaraweeh\\b","\\bnight prayers? (?:in|of) ramadan\\b","\\bramadan night prayers?\\b"],t.s)
B.aVd=s(["\\b(?:app|screen|guide|reminder)\\b"],t.s)
B.ame=new A.aN("taraweeh",B.bmh,B.aVd,"Taraweeh is the extra night prayer of Ramadan, prayed after Isha, usually in congregation at the masjid. It is a strongly recommended sunnah, not an obligation. It is prayed two rak'ahs at a time.\n\nHow many rak'ahs: the schools and communities differ, and all of it is accepted.\n- Eight rak'ahs plus witr (eleven in all): this follows A'ishah's account that the Prophet \ufdfa never prayed more than eleven rak'ahs at night, in Ramadan or outside it (Sahih al-Bukhari 1147).\n- Twenty rak'ahs plus witr: the view of the Hanafi, Shafi'i and Hanbali schools, and what many masjids follow. Praying taraweeh together behind one reciter goes back to Umar, who gathered the people for it in Ramadan (Sahih al-Bukhari 2010).\n\nYou may pray it at home alone if you cannot reach the masjid. Praying longer with a calm, unhurried recitation matters more than the number.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1147, 2010.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-taraweeh]","taraweeh",!0,"")
B.aYB=s(["\\beid prayer\\b","\\bprayer (?:of|for|on) eid\\b","\\bhow (?:to|do (?:i|we|you)) pray eid\\b","\\beid (?:salah|namaz)\\b","\\bpray eid\\b","\\beid prayer (?:rakah|steps)\\b"],t.s)
B.aRI=s(["\\b(?:app|screen|game|date|when is)\\b"],t.s)
B.alr=new A.aN("eid-prayer",B.aYB,B.aRI,"The Eid prayer (for Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha) is two rak'ahs prayed in congregation, usually in an open ground or large masjid, after the sun has risen about a spear's length. There is no adhan and no iqamah.\n\nHow it goes:\n1. Make the intention and say the opening takbir.\n2. Extra takbirs: the Maliki, Shafi'i and Hanbali schools say seven takbirs in the first rak'ah (after the opening) and five in the second, raising the hands with each. The Hanafi school says three extra takbirs in each rak'ah.\n3. Recite al-Fatihah and a surah, then finish each rak'ah as in any prayer.\n4. After the salam, the imam gives the khutbah. The Prophet \ufdfa prayed first and gave the khutbah after (Sahih al-Bukhari 957).\n\nRuling: the Hanafi school calls it wajib, the Maliki and Shafi'i schools a strongly recommended sunnah, and the Hanbali school a communal duty (fard kifayah). Women are encouraged to attend; those who are menstruating stay out of the prayer area but share in the du'a.\n\nBefore the Eid al-Fitr prayer it is a sunnah to eat some dates: the Prophet \ufdfa never went out on that day until he had eaten some (Sahih al-Bukhari 953). Zakat al-fitr is paid before people go out to the prayer (Sahih al-Bukhari 1503).\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 957, 953, 1503.","eid",!0,"")
B.aWt=s(["\\bjumuah (?:prayer )?(?:rakah|how many)\\b","\\bhow many rakah\\b.*\\bjumuah\\b","\\bwhat is jumuah\\b","\\bis jumuah (?:obligatory|fard|compulsory|wajib|mandatory)\\b","\\bjumuah (?:obligatory|fard|compulsory|mandatory)\\b","\\bmust (?:i|women|woman) (?:attend|go to|pray) jumuah\\b","\\bwomen\\b.*\\bjumuah\\b","\\bfriday prayers?\\b","\\bjumuah\\b"],t.s)
B.b8j=s(["\\b(?:masjid|masajid|mosque|mosques|near me|nearest|closest|nearby|times?|timings?|schedule)\\b","\\b(?:app|screen|game|guide|khutbah|khutba|sermon)\\b"],t.s)
B.alt=new A.aN("jumuah-basics",B.aWt,B.b8j,"Jumu'ah (the Friday prayer) is two rak'ahs, prayed in congregation at the time of Dhuhr, in place of Dhuhr. Before it, the imam gives two short sermons (khutbahs), and listening to them is part of the prayer.\n\nWho must attend: every free adult Muslim man who is a resident (not travelling) and is able to go. When the call is made on Friday, the Qur'an says: \"then proceed to the remembrance of Allah and leave trade\" (Qur'an 62:9). Women, travellers, the sick and children are not required to attend, but a woman may go if she wishes, and if she prays at home she prays Dhuhr (four rak'ahs).\n\nSunnah acts of the day: ghusl, clean clothes, perfume (for men), going early, reciting Surah al-Kahf, and sending more blessings on the Prophet \ufdfa.\n\nIf you miss Jumu'ah, pray Dhuhr. Neglecting it is serious: the Prophet \ufdfa said people must stop neglecting the Friday prayer or Allah will seal their hearts (Sahih Muslim 865).\n\nSource: Qur'an 62:9; Sahih Muslim 865.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-jumuah]","jumuah",!0,"")
B.aLd=s(["\\bshorten(?:ed|ing)? (?:the )?prayers?\\b","\\bqasr\\b","\\bshortened prayers?\\b","\\b(?:travel|traveling|travelling|traveller|traveler|journey|trip|safar)\\b.*\\bprayers?\\b","\\bprayers?\\b.*\\b(?:travel|traveling|travelling|traveller|traveler|journey|trip|safar|airplane|plane|flight)\\b","\\bcombin\\w*\\b.*\\b(?:prayers?|dhuhr|zuhr|asr|maghrib|isha)\\b","\\b(?:dhuhr|zuhr|asr|maghrib|isha)\\b.*\\b(?:and|with)\\b.*\\b(?:together|combined|joined)\\b.*\\b(?:travel\\w*|trip|journey)\\b","\\bjam\\b","\\bpray(?:ing)? (?:on|in) (?:a |the )?(?:plane|airplane|train|bus|car|ship|boat)\\b"],t.s)
B.b14=s(["\\b(?:times? (?:today|now|tomorrow)|app screen|game)\\b"],t.s)
B.and=new A.aN("travel-prayer",B.aLd,B.b14,"A traveller may shorten the four-rak'ah prayers (Dhuhr, Asr and Isha) to two rak'ahs. Fajr (2) and Maghrib (3) stay as they are. This is a mercy from Allah: \"there is no blame upon you for shortening the prayer\" (Qur'an 4:101). Umar asked the Prophet \ufdfa about it, and he said it is a charity from Allah, so accept His charity (Sahih Muslim 686).\n\nWhen it applies: on a journey of about 80 km (roughly 50 miles) or more, by the view of most scholars. Some scholars say there is no fixed distance. You begin shortening when you leave your town. How long you can keep shortening in one place differs: the Shafi'i, Maliki and Hanbali schools say up to about four days of staying; the Hanafi school says up to fifteen days.\n\nCombining: the Shafi'i, Maliki and Hanbali schools allow a traveller to join Dhuhr with Asr, and Maghrib with Isha, at the time of either. The Prophet \ufdfa did this, delaying Dhuhr to Asr when he set out before noon (Sahih al-Bukhari 1111). The Hanafi school allows joining only at Arafah and Muzdalifah during Hajj. You may not skip a prayer or push it out of its day.\n\nOn a plane or train: face the qibla as far as you can and pray seated if you cannot stand. If the time will run out, pray in your seat; do not leave a prayer to be made up unless you have to.\n\nSource: Qur'an 4:101; Sahih Muslim 686; Sahih al-Bukhari 1111.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-travel]","travel prayer",!0,"")
B.bnF=s(["\\bpray(?:ing)? (?:sitting|seated|lying|while sitting|in a chair|on a chair|from a chair)\\b","\\bsit(?:ting)? down\\b.*\\bpray\\b","\\bpray\\b.*\\bsit(?:ting)? down\\b","\\b(?:sick|ill|illness|injured|elderly|disabled|disability|wheelchair|bed ?ridden|back pain|knee pain|surgery|pregnan\\w*)\\b.*\\bprayers?\\b","\\bprayers?\\b.*\\b(?:when|if) (?:i am|im|i m) (?:sick|ill|injured|unwell|pregnant)\\b","\\bcannot stand\\b.*\\bprayers?\\b","\\bcant stand\\b.*\\bprayers?\\b","\\bprayers? (?:in|on) (?:a )?(?:chair|wheelchair|bed)\\b","\\bprayers?\\b.*\\bunable to stand\\b","\\bprayers?\\b.*\\bsick(?:ness)?\\b"],t.s)
