B.alb=new A.aN("zakat-amount",B.bl9,B.aQg,'The rate is 2.5% (one fortieth) of your zakatable wealth, once it has reached the nisab and a lunar year has passed on it. For example, if your zakatable wealth on your zakat date is 10,000 and that is above the nisab, you pay 250.\n\nThe nisab is the minimum: the value of 85 grams of gold or 595 grams of silver. The Prophet \ufdfa set the silver measure at five awqiyah, which is 200 dirhams (Sahih al-Bukhari 1447). Silver is the lower figure, so more people reach it and more of the poor are helped. Many scholars today use the silver measure; some use gold. The Zakat calculator lets you choose, and can fill in today\'s gold and silver prices.\n\nZakat is paid on what is left after the debts you owe that are due, if you follow the Hanafi, Maliki or Hanbali view; ask me "what about debts" for that. Crops and livestock have their own rates; ask a scholar.\n\nFor your own figure, open the Zakat calculator. It keeps your numbers on your device.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1447.\n[GO:/zakat]\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-zakat]',"zakat",!0,"")
B.blw=s(["\\bnisab\\b","\\bhow much zakat\\b","\\bhow (?:much|many) (?:is|do i|should i|to|zakat)\\b.*\\bzakat\\b","\\bzakat\\b.*\\bhow much\\b","\\bzakat (?:rate|amount|percentage|percent)\\b","\\b(?:2.5|two and a half)\\b.*\\bzakat\\b","\\bzakat\\b.*\\b(?:2.5|two and a half)\\b","\\bwho (?:must|has to|should|needs to) pay zakat\\b","\\bwho is (?:required|obliged) to pay zakat\\b","\\bcalculate zakat\\b","\\bhow to (?:calculate|work out|pay) zakat\\b","\\bwhat is zakat\\b","\\bzakat on (?:gold|silver|savings|money|cash|jewel\\w*|business|stocks?|shares?|property|house|car|salary|crypto|investments?|rental|land|pension|retirement)\\b","\\bis zakat (?:due|payable|required|obligatory) on\\b","\\bzakat (?:and|for) (?:gold|silver|savings|jewel\\w*)\\b","\\bdo i (?:pay|owe) zakat\\b"],t.s)
B.bfG=s(["\\b(?:fitr|recipients?)\\b"],t.s)
B.amW=new A.aN("zakat-nisab-rate",B.blw,B.bfG,"Zakat is the third pillar of Islam: an obligatory charity on wealth, given to those in need. It purifies your wealth and soul. Allah pairs it with prayer again and again, for example \"establish prayer and give zakah\" (Qur'an 2:43).\n\nWho must pay: every adult, sane Muslim whose savings reach the nisab (minimum threshold) and who has held them for a full lunar year (the hawl), after deducting debts that are due.\n\nThe nisab is the value of 85 grams of gold or 595 grams of silver. The Prophet \ufdfa set the silver nisab at five awqiyah, which is 200 dirhams (Sahih al-Bukhari 1447). The gold nisab is 20 dinars, about 85 grams. Many scholars today use the silver measure, because it is lower and helps the poor; the Zakat calculator lets you choose.\n\nThe rate is 2.5% of the zakatable wealth on the day your year is complete.\n\nWhat counts: cash, savings and money owed to you that you can collect, gold and silver (bullion, coins and stored jewelry), business stock and goods held for sale, and shares or investments held for trade. Your home, your car and tools for your work are not zakatable. Worn jewelry is a school matter (the Hanafi school counts it; the Maliki, Shafi'i and Hanbali schools do not).\n\nThe Zakat screen in this app works out the nisab and your zakat with these rules. It keeps your numbers on your device. For a complex case (pensions, a business, shares, rent), ask a scholar.\n\nSource: Qur'an 2:43; Sahih al-Bukhari 1447.\n[GO:/zakat]\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-zakat]","zakat",!0,"")
B.aTR=s(["\\b(?:woman|women|female|lady|ladies|wife|girl|sister|mother)\\b.*\\b(?:hajj|umrah)\\b.*\\b(?:mahram|without|alone|husband|group|travel)\\b","\\b(?:hajj|umrah)\\b.*\\b(?:mahram)\\b","\\bmahram\\b.*\\b(?:hajj|umrah|travel)\\b","\\b(?:woman|women|female|lady)\\b.*\\b(?:travel\\w*)\\b.*\\b(?:mahram|alone|without)\\b","\\btravel\\w* without (?:a )?mahram\\b","\\bwoman travel\\w*\\b","\\bwomen travel\\w*\\b","\\bwhat is (?:a )?mahram\\b","\\bwho is (?:a )?mahram\\b","\\bmahram\\b"],t.s)
B.amf=new A.aN("hajj-mahram",B.aTR,B.b,"A mahram is a man a woman can never marry because of blood, marriage or nursing (such as her father, brother, son, uncle, or her husband). The Prophet \ufdfa said that a woman should not travel except with a mahram (Sahih al-Bukhari 1862) (Sahih Muslim 1341).\n\nOn Hajj and Umrah, the schools differ:\n- The Hanafi and Hanbali schools make the mahram a condition: a woman without one is not required to go on Hajj, and should not go.\n- The Shafi'i and Maliki schools allow a woman to go in a safe group of trustworthy women when the road is safe, as a condition of ability.\n- Today many scholars and authorities allow a woman to join an organised, safe pilgrimage group, relying on the safety of modern travel, while others still require the mahram. The authorities in charge of the pilgrimage also set their own entry rules, so check them before you book.\n\nThis is a real question with different opinions, so a woman in this position should ask a scholar she trusts. The Hajj is for those who are able (\"to Allah from the people is a pilgrimage to the House - for whoever is able to find thereto a way\" (Qur'an 3:97)).\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1862; Sahih Muslim 1341; Qur'an 3:97.\n[GO:/academy/hajj-umrah-pack]","mahram",!0,"")
B.bdq=s(["\\bdays? of hajj\\b","\\bhajj (?:days?|steps?|rites?|rituals?|schedule|timeline|itinerary|order|process|procedure)\\b","\\bwhat (?:happens|do (?:pilgrims|muslims|they) do) (?:on|during|in) hajj\\b","\\bsteps? (?:of|in|for) hajj\\b","\\bhow (?:to|do (?:i|we|you)) (?:perform|do) hajj\\b","\\brites? of hajj\\b","\\bpillars? of hajj\\b","\\bhow is hajj performed\\b","\\bwhen is hajj\\b","\\bwhich month is hajj\\b","\\bday of arafah\\b","\\barafah\\b","\\bmuzdalifah\\b","\\bmina\\b","\\bjamarat\\b","\\bstoning of the devil\\b","\\btawaf\\b","\\bsa i\\b","\\bsai\\b","\\bihram\\b","\\bmiqat\\b","\\btamattu\\b","\\bwhat is hajj\\b","\\bhajj (?:explained|basics|guide|for beginners)\\b"],t.s)
B.bnT=s(["\\b(?:difference|vs|versus|compare)\\b","\\b(?:mahram|woman|women|female)\\b"],t.s)
B.ale=new A.aN("hajj-days",B.bdq,B.bnT,"Hajj is the pilgrimage to Makkah, the fifth pillar of Islam, obligatory once in a lifetime for every Muslim who is able. Allah says: \"to Allah from the people is a pilgrimage to the House - for whoever is able to find thereto a way\" (Qur'an 3:97). When the Prophet \ufdfa told people it was obligatory and someone asked if it was every year, he stayed silent and then said that if he had said yes it would have been required, and it would have been too hard (Sahih Muslim 1337)\n\nHajj has fixed days in the month of Dhul Hijjah (the twelfth month):\n- 8 Dhul Hijjah (Yawm at-Tarwiyah): enter ihram (for those in Makkah) and go to Mina, praying and spending the day and night there.\n- 9 Dhul Hijjah (the Day of Arafah): stand at Arafah from after noon until sunset, in du'a and dhikr. This is the heart of Hajj; without it there is no Hajj. After sunset, go to Muzdalifah, pray Maghrib and Isha together, sleep there and collect pebbles.\n- 10 Dhul Hijjah (Eid al-Adha): after Fajr, go to Mina and throw seven pebbles at the large pillar (Jamrat al-Aqabah), offer the sacrifice, shave or trim the hair (men), and go to Makkah for Tawaf al-Ifadah and the Sa'i of Safa and Marwah.\n- 11, 12 (and 13 for those who stay) Dhul Hijjah: in Mina, throw seven pebbles at each of the three pillars each day after midday. Those in a hurry may leave on the 12th before sunset.\n- Farewell tawaf at the Kaaba before leaving Makkah.\n\nThe main pillars (arkan) are ihram, standing at Arafah, Tawaf al-Ifadah and the Sa'i (the Hanafi school counts the Sa'i as wajib instead). There are three forms: Tamattu' (Umrah first, then Hajj), Qiran (both together) and Ifrad (Hajj alone). The Prophet \ufdfa said that whoever performs Hajj for Allah's pleasure and avoids bad conduct returns as if newly born (Sahih al-Bukhari 1521).\n\nThe Hajj guides in this app cover each step in detail.\n\nSource: Qur'an 3:97; Sahih Muslim 1337; Sahih al-Bukhari 1521.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-hajj-overview]\n[GO:/academy/hajj-umrah-pack]","hajj",!0,"")
B.bjv=s(["\\bhajj\\b.*\\bumrah\\b.*\\b(?:difference|different|same|vs|versus|compare|between)\\b","\\b(?:difference|different|between)\\b.*\\bhajj\\b.*\\bumrah\\b","\\bumrah\\b.*\\bhajj\\b.*\\b(?:difference|different|same|vs|versus|compare|between)\\b","\\bwhat is umrah\\b","\\bumrah (?:steps?|rites?|rituals?|explained|guide|basics|how|process)\\b","\\bhow (?:to|do (?:i|we|you)) (?:perform|do) umrah\\b","\\bsmall hajj\\b","\\blesser pilgrimage\\b","\\bumrah (?:compulsory|obligatory|fard|mandatory|required|sunnah)\\b","\\bis umrah (?:compulsory|obligatory|fard|mandatory|required|sunnah)\\b"],t.s)
B.alo=new A.aN("hajj-vs-umrah",B.bjv,B.b,"Umrah is the \"lesser pilgrimage\": a shorter, voluntary journey to the Kaaba that can be done at any time of the year. Hajj is the major pilgrimage with fixed days, obligatory once on every Muslim who is able.\n\nUmrah has four parts: (1) enter ihram at the miqat with the intention for Umrah, (2) circle the Kaaba seven times (tawaf), (3) walk between Safa and Marwah seven times (sa'i), (4) shave or trim the hair (men shave or trim; women trim a fingertip's length). That is all; it takes a few hours.\n\nHajj includes similar acts (ihram, tawaf, sa'i) but adds standing at Arafah, the night at Muzdalifah, stoning the pillars in Mina, and the sacrifice, over five or six fixed days in Dhul Hijjah.\n\nThe difference in ruling: Hajj is a pillar of Islam. Umrah is obligatory once for those able in the Shafi'i and Hanbali schools, and a strongly recommended sunnah in the Hanafi and Maliki schools. The Prophet \ufdfa said that one Umrah is an expiation for the sins between it and the previous one, and the reward of an accepted Hajj is nothing less than Paradise (Sahih al-Bukhari 1773). Umrah does not replace Hajj.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1773.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-umrah]\n[GO:/academy/hajj-umrah-pack]","umrah",!0,"")
B.aZn=s(["\\bcredit cards?\\b","\\bcharge cards?\\b"],t.s)
B.aZ4=s(["\\b(?:debt|nisab|zakat)\\b"],t.s)
B.alW=new A.aN("credit-card",B.aZn,B.aZ4,"A credit card is a loan tool, so the answer depends on how it is used, not on the plastic. The problem is interest (riba): \"Allah has permitted trade and has forbidden interest\" (Qur'an 2:275), and the Prophet \ufdfa cursed everyone involved in it (Sahih Muslim 1598).\n\n- Carrying a balance that is charged interest is riba, and it is not allowed.\n- Paying the full balance before any interest is charged: many contemporary scholars allow having and using the card on that basis, as long as you are sure you can pay in time and you do not take on interest or late charges you could avoid.\n- Some scholars are stricter and say not to take a conventional card at all, because the agreement you sign states interest on late payment.\n- Safer options: a debit card, a charge card that must be paid in full each month, or a card from an Islamic provider where one exists.\n\nWhich view to follow in your own case is for a qualified scholar. The Zakat guide in this app walks the pillar that keeps wealth clean.\n\nSource: Qur'an 2:275; Sahih Muslim 1598.\n[GO:/zakat]","credit cards",!0,"")
B.aMC=s(["\\briba\\b","\\binterest\\b.*\\b(?:haram|halal|islam|allowed|permissible|forbidden|prohibited|sin|bank|loan|mortgage|savings|account|credit)\\b","\\b(?:haram|halal|islam|allowed|permissible|forbidden|prohibited|sin)\\b.*\\binterest\\b","\\busury\\b","\\bbank interest\\b","\\binterest (?:on|from|in) (?:my )?(?:savings|bank|account|loan|deposit)\\b","\\bwhat to do with (?:bank )?interest\\b","\\bsavings account\\b","\\bcredit cards?\\b","\\bpersonal loans?\\b","\\bstudent loans?\\b","\\bcar loans?\\b","\\bis (?:it )?(?:haram|halal|ok|okay|allowed) to (?:take|get|have|use) (?:a )?(?:loan|credit)\\b"],t.s)
B.aNF=s(["\\b(?:mortgage|house loan|home loan)\\b"],t.s)
B.alL=new A.aN("riba-interest",B.aMC,B.aNF,'Riba (interest, usury) is forbidden in Islam. Allah says: "Allah has permitted trade and has forbidden interest" (Qur\'an 2:275), and "fear Allah and give up what remains [due to you] of interest, if you should be believers" (Qur\'an 2:278-279). The Prophet \ufdfa cursed the one who takes interest, the one who pays it, the one who records it, and the two witnesses (Sahih Muslim 1598).\n\nWhat this covers: interest on loans and credit cards, interest on savings accounts and fixed deposits, and any agreement where a loan carries an extra amount stated in advance. Paying a late fee that grows with time is interest too.\n\nWhat to do:\n- Borrow only when needed, from interest-free sources first (family, a qard hasan loan from a community fund, an employer).\n- Use a halal alternative: Islamic banks and finance firms (profit-sharing, lease-to-own, cost-plus sale), where available.\n- A credit card that you pay off in full every month, so no interest is ever charged, is considered acceptable by many scholars, though some avoid cards with interest-bearing terms; ask a scholar.\n- Interest your bank puts into your account: take it out and give it away to the poor or for public good, with no intention of reward for yourself (the view of most scholars today). Do not keep it. If you can, move to an interest-free account.\n- If you are already in a loan with interest, you do not tear up the contract, but you repent, pay it off as soon as you can, and avoid new ones.\n\nFor mortgages, see the separate answer. Ask a scholar for your own situation. The Zakat guide in this app walks the pillar that keeps wealth clean.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:275, 2:278-279; Sahih Muslim 1598.\n[GO:/zakat]',"riba",!0,"")
B.bkM=s(["\\bmortgage\\w*\\b","\\bhome loans?\\b","\\bhouse loans?\\b","\\bbuy(?:ing)? (?:a |my )?(?:house|home|property|flat|apartment)\\b","\\bhome financing\\b","\\bislamic mortgage\\b","\\bhalal mortgage\\b","\\bhome purchase\\b"],t.s)
B.alT=new A.aN("mortgage",B.bkM,B.b,"A conventional mortgage charges interest, and interest (riba) is forbidden (Qur'an 2:275), so most scholars say a conventional mortgage is not allowed.\n\nMuslims living in Western countries are divided. Some contemporary scholars and councils allow a conventional mortgage as an exception when it is a real need (a family home, no halal option available, renting is not a realistic way to house the family), on the principle of necessity in Islamic law. Others say that necessity here is rare, the prohibition is firm, and renting is possible, so a conventional mortgage is not allowed. This is a genuine disagreement among scholars, and I will not pick one for you.\n\nThe widely accepted route is a halal home finance product: a diminishing partnership (musharakah), lease-to-own (ijarah) or cost-plus (murabaha) arrangement from an Islamic finance provider. These are structured so that no loan interest is charged. Ask a scholar to check the contract before you sign.\n\nMeanwhile, saving for a larger deposit or renting a home is always allowed. For your own decision, please talk to a qualified scholar who knows the local options.\n\nSource: Qur'an 2:275.\n[GO:/zakat]","mortgage",!0,"")
B.aZ2=s(["\\b(?:bitcoin|btc|ethereum|eth|crypto\\w*|altcoin\\w*|nft\\w*|dogecoin|token\\w*)\\b","\\b(?:stock|stocks|share|shares|equities|investing|investment|investments|day trading|forex|trading|options trading|etf|etfs|mutual funds?|index funds?|dividends?)\\b.*\\b(?:halal|haram|allowed|permissible|islam\\w*|sharia|shariah|forbidden)\\b","\\b(?:halal|haram|allowed|permissible|islam\\w*|sharia|shariah|forbidden)\\b.*\\b(?:stock|stocks|share|shares|equities|investing|investment|investments|day trading|forex|trading|options|etf|etfs|mutual funds?|index funds?|dividends?)\\b","\\b(?:insurance|takaful)\\b","\\bshort ?selling\\b","\\bmargin\\b.*\\btrading\\b"],t.s)
B.alH=new A.aN("crypto-stocks-insurance",B.aZ2,B.b,"Bitcoin and other crypto: scholars disagree. Some say it is permitted as a commodity or digital asset, subject to the usual rules (no fraud, no interest, no gambling-style speculation). Others say it is not allowed because it has no real backing, is highly uncertain (gharar), and is mostly used for speculation. Because of this real difference, please ask a scholar you trust before you buy; do not buy something you do not understand.\n\nStocks and shares: owning a share in a real business is generally permitted by most contemporary scholars, if the business itself is halal (not alcohol, pork, gambling, interest-based banking, adult entertainment) and the share is not mostly debt-funded or interest-earning. Many halal screening services and Islamic funds exist for this. Day trading, margin trading, short selling, and options are widely seen as speculative or involving interest, and many scholars forbid them.\n\nInsurance: conventional insurance has uncertainty and an element of gambling, and many scholars forbid it, while allowing what the law requires (such as car insurance) because of need. Takaful (cooperative insurance) is the Islamic alternative.\n\nEverything here is general, and these are areas where scholarly opinions differ. Check with a qualified scholar or an Islamic finance advisor for your own money. The Zakat guide in this app walks the pillar that keeps wealth clean.\n[GO:/zakat]","investing",!0,"")
B.bak=s(["\\b(?:gambl\\w*|lotter\\w*|casino\\w*|betting|bet|bets|raffle\\w*|poker|scratch cards?|sweepstake\\w*|maysir|wager\\w*|sports betting|loot box\\w*)\\b"],t.s)
B.aVt=s(["\\b(?:game|games|story|stories)\\b.*\\b(?:play|ludo|chess)\\b"],t.s)
B.ald=new A.aN("gambling-lottery",B.bak,B.aVt,"Gambling is forbidden in Islam. Allah says: \"intoxicants, gambling, [sacrificing on] stone alters [to other than Allah], and divining arrows are but defilement from the work of Satan, so avoid it that you may be successful\" (Qur'an 5:90). In another verse, \"In them is great sin and [yet, some] benefit for people. But their sin is greater than their benefit\" (Qur'an 2:219).\n\nThis includes casinos, betting on sports, lotteries, raffles where you pay and may win a prize, scratch cards, and games where you pay real money for chance outcomes (including loot boxes, according to many scholars). A prize won through gambling is haram money.\n\nWhat is allowed: a fair prize in a skill contest where the prize is paid by a sponsor (not from the entrants' fees), and games for fun with no stake. If you have won money by gambling, many scholars say to give it away to the poor without expecting reward, and to repent and stop.\n\nSource: Qur'an 5:90, 2:219.\n[GO:/zakat]","gambling",!0,"")
B.bok=s(["\\bpork\\b","\\bpig\\b","\\bpigs\\b","\\bswine\\b","\\bbacon\\b","\\bham\\b","\\bhot ?dogs?\\b.*\\b(?:halal|haram|eat|allowed)\\b","\\blard\\b","\\bpepperoni\\b","\\bwhy is pork\\b","\\bwhy (?:cant|cant|do) muslims? (?:not )?eat pork\\b"],t.s)
B.anb=new A.aN("pork",B.bok,B.b,"Pork is forbidden for Muslims. Allah says: \"He has only forbidden to you dead animals, blood, the flesh of swine, and that which has been dedicated to other than Allah\" (Qur'an 2:173). The same is said in Qur'an 5:3 and Qur'an 6:145. This covers the meat and fat (lard) of the pig and foods made from them, such as bacon, ham and pepperoni.\n\nPlease also check labels: gelatin, some enzymes, emulsifiers and shortening can come from pigs unless the label says halal, vegetable or beef. See the answer on gelatin.\n\nThe exception is necessity: the same verse says \"whoever is forced [by necessity], neither desiring [it] nor transgressing [its limit], there is no sin upon him\" (Qur'an 2:173). If you eat it by mistake without knowing, there is no sin; just stop when you find out.\n\nSource: Qur'an 2:173, 5:3, 6:145.","pork",!0,"")
B.aOJ=s(["\\balcohol\\b","\\bwine\\b","\\bbeer\\b","\\bliquor\\b","\\bwhisky\\b","\\bwhiskey\\b","\\bvodka\\b","\\bintoxicant\\w*\\b","\\bkhamr\\b","\\bdrinking\\b.*\\b(?:haram|halal|allowed|islam)\\b","\\bdrunk\\b","\\b(?:hand )?sanitizer\\b","\\bvanilla (?:extract|essence)\\b","\\bcook\\w* with (?:wine|alcohol)\\b","\\bnon ?alcoholic (?:beer|wine|drinks?)\\b","\\balcohol[- ]free\\b","\\bperfume\\b.*\\balcohol\\b","\\bkombucha\\b"],t.s)
B.b3c=s(["\\b(?:game|story|stories)\\b"],t.s)
B.amF=new A.aN("alcohol",B.aOJ,B.b3c,'Alcohol is forbidden in Islam. Allah says: "intoxicants, gambling, [sacrificing on] stone alters [to other than Allah], and divining arrows are but defilement from the work of Satan, so avoid it that you may be successful" (Qur\'an 5:90). The Prophet \ufdfa said: every intoxicant is khamr and every intoxicant is forbidden (Sahih Muslim 2003). This includes beer, wine, spirits, and drugs that intoxicate.\n\nQuestions people often have:\n- Cooking with alcohol: avoid it; if it is a recipe that keeps the alcohol in the dish, the dish is forbidden. Many scholars do not allow cooking it away either.\n- Hand sanitiser and perfume containing alcohol: used on the outside, most contemporary scholars allow them, since the ruling is on drinking and the alcohol is not for intoxication. Some prefer alcohol-free.\n- Non-alcoholic ("0.0%") drinks that taste like beer or wine: scholars differ. Some allow them if truly under about 0.5% and not intoxicating; others dislike them because of the name and imitation. A cautious person avoids them.\n- Vinegar, including vinegar that began as wine and has fully turned to vinegar: most scholars allow it, though some dislike making it from wine on purpose. The Prophet \ufdfa said the best of condiments is vinegar (Sahih Muslim 2051).\n- Tiny traces from natural fermentation in food (a ripe fruit, bread, or vinegar) are not what is meant.\n- Medicine: if there is no alternative, medicine containing alcohol is allowed by necessity; ask your doctor for a non-alcoholic form.\n\nIf you slipped, repent and do not return. Allah is forgiving (Qur\'an 39:53).\n\nIf you drink now and want to stop:\n- Start today. Repentance is to leave it, to regret it, and to decide not to go back, and Allah says "do not despair of the mercy of Allah. Indeed, Allah forgives all sins" (Qur\'an 39:53). Pour it away, keep none at home, and tell one person you trust.\n- If you drink a lot or every day, do not stop all at once on your own. Sudden withdrawal from heavy drinking can cause seizures and can kill. Speak to a doctor or a pharmacist today and tell them how much you drink; they can help you stop safely. Scholars apply "do not kill yourselves" (Qur\'an 4:29) to protecting your life, and accept the treatment a doctor prescribes to get through withdrawal. Look after your health first, and keep the intention to leave it.\n- You do not have to do this alone. The "Need help now" button at the top of this chat shows help lines for your country, and the Support and wellbeing page has a page on alcohol. You can also tell me how it is going.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 5:90, 39:53, 4:29; Sahih Muslim 2003, 2051.\n[GO:/support]',"alcohol",!0,"")
B.aRu=s(["\\b(?:shrimp|prawn|prawns|crab|lobster|oyster|oysters|clam|clams|mussel|mussels|squid|calamari|octopus|shellfish|seafood|sea food|eel|catfish|crustacean\\w*|scallop\\w*|fish)\\b.*\\b(?:halal|haram|allowed|permissible|permitted|eat|forbidden|makruh|islam)\\b","\\b(?:halal|haram|allowed|permissible|permitted|forbidden|makruh)\\b.*\\b(?:shrimp|prawn|prawns|crab|lobster|oyster|oysters|clam|clams|mussel|mussels|squid|calamari|octopus|shellfish|seafood|sea food|eel|catfish|crustacean\\w*|scallop\\w*|fish)\\b"],t.s)
B.an2=new A.aN("seafood-shrimp",B.aRu,B.b,"Most of the seafood you ask about is halal. The Qur'an says \"Lawful to you is game from the sea and its food as provision for you and the travelers\" (Qur'an 5:96). When a man asked the Prophet \ufdfa about wudu with sea water, he said its water is pure and what dies in it is lawful to eat (Sunan Abu Dawud 83, sahih per al-Albani).\n\nThe schools differ on the detail:\n- The Maliki, Shafi'i and Hanbali schools: all sea animals are halal (with some exceptions for harmful ones in some views). That includes fish, shrimp, prawns, crab, lobster, squid and octopus.\n- The Hanafi school: fish only is halal without question. Shrimp and prawns are considered fish by many Hanafi scholars and are permitted; other shellfish such as crab and lobster are disliked or forbidden for strict Hanafis, and many Hanafi scholars today allow them.\n\nSo shrimp is halal in all the schools according to most scholars; if you follow the Hanafi school strictly, ask your scholar about crab, lobster and other shellfish. Seafood cooked in alcohol or fried in oil also used for pork is another matter.\n\nSource: Qur'an 5:96; Sunan Abu Dawud 83, sahih per al-Albani.","seafood",!0,"")
B.b0e=s(["\\bgelatin\\w*\\b","\\bgelatine\\b","\\bmarshmallows?\\b","\\bgummy\\b","\\bgummies\\b","\\bjello\\b","\\bjelly\\b.*\\b(?:halal|haram)\\b","\\bcapsules?\\b.*\\b(?:gelatin|halal|haram)\\b","\\bvaccin\\w*\\b.*\\b(?:pork|gelatin|haram|halal)\\b","\\bhidden (?:haram|ingredients?)\\b","\\bfood (?:additives?|e numbers?|ingredients?)\\b","\\be ?[0-9]{3}\\b.*\\b(?:halal|haram)\\b","\\bhow (?:do|to) (?:i )?check (?:if )?(?:food|a product|ingredients?) (?:is )?halal\\b","\\bis this halal\\b"],t.s)
B.alO=new A.aN("gelatin",B.b0e,B.b,"Gelatin is made from animal skin and bones, usually pork or beef. Pork is forbidden (Qur'an 2:173), so:\n- Pork gelatin is haram.\n- Gelatin from beef or fish is halal if the cattle were slaughtered in the Islamic way (for beef) or it is from fish.\n- Gelatin when the source is not stated: scholars differ. Some (including several fiqh councils) hold that the bones and skin undergo a complete chemical change (istihalah) and so the final product is pure and lawful; others say pork remains pork, so it is not allowed. Because of this real difference, the careful course is to avoid gelatin of unknown source and to choose products marked halal, fish gelatin, pectin or agar.\n\nHow to check: look for a halal logo from a certifier you trust; \"gelatin\" alone does not tell you the animal. Other hidden items include alcohol in flavourings, pork-derived enzymes, lard and some emulsifiers (E441 is gelatin; E120, carmine, is from insects and disputed). Marshmallows, gummy sweets, jelly desserts and some capsules and vaccines commonly contain gelatin.\n\nFor medicines and vaccines, many scholars allow them even if gelatin of uncertain source is in them, when there is a need and no alternative; ask your doctor and a scholar.\n\nSource: Qur'an 2:173.","gelatin",!0,"")
B.b2L=s(["\\bhalal meat\\b","\\bzabiha\\b","\\bzabihah\\b","\\bdhabiha\\b","\\bslaughter\\w*\\b","\\bstunn\\w*\\b","\\bkosher\\b","\\bsupermarket meat\\b","\\bmeat from (?:a )?(?:non muslim|christian|jewish|supermarket|restaurant)\\b","\\bnon muslim\\b.*\\b(?:meat|food|slaughter)\\b","\\b(?:meat|food)\\b.*\\b(?:people of the book|ahl al kitab|christians?|jews?)\\b","\\bbismillah\\b.*\\b(?:eat|meat|slaughter)\\b","\\bhalal (?:food|restaurant|restaurants|certification|certified)\\b","\\beat(?:ing)? (?:at|in) (?:a )?(?:non muslim|restaurant|mcdonalds|kfc|fast food)\\b","\\bwhat makes (?:meat|food) halal\\b","\\bhow (?:do|can|to) (?:i|we|you) (?:know|tell|check|find out) (?:if |whether )?(?:the )?(?:meat|food|chicken|beef|this|it) (?:is )?halal\\b","\\bhow to know (?:if )?(?:meat|food) is halal\\b","\\bis (?:kfc|mcdonalds|subway|fast food) halal\\b","\\bchicken\\b.*\\bhalal\\b"],t.s)
B.aWW=s(["\\b(?:near me|nearby|app|find)\\b"],t.s)
B.alV=new A.aN("halal-meat",B.b2L,B.aWW,'Meat is halal when the animal is a permitted kind (cattle, sheep, goat, camel, chicken, deer, etc. - not pig, carnivores, or dead animals), and it is slaughtered properly: by a Muslim (or one of the People of the Book), with a sharp knife cutting the throat, saying "Bismillah, Allahu akbar" at the time, so that the blood flows. The Qur\'an forbids dead animals, blood, pork and what is dedicated to other than Allah (Qur\'an 5:3).\n\nWhat about meat from non-Muslims? The Qur\'an says "the food of those who were given the Scripture is lawful for you" (Qur\'an 5:5). The majority of scholars take this to mean the meat of Jews and Christians who slaughter in their religion\'s way is allowed. But many say today\'s industrial slaughter, which may not follow that, is not covered. So:\n- Safest: buy meat that is certified halal.\n- Kosher meat is accepted by many scholars as it follows a similar slaughter, though some require "Bismillah".\n- Supermarket or restaurant meat of unknown slaughter: scholars differ. Some allow it based on the verse above, citing the general rule that food is lawful unless known to be unlawful. Others say it is not allowed unless known to be halal. Following the stricter view is the common practice.\n- Stunning: allowed by many scholars if the animal is alive at the cut and would survive the stun; others reject it. Machine slaughter with a recorded "Bismillah" is also debated.\n- Fish and seafood do not need slaughter.\n\nIf you find yourself eating non-halal meat by mistake or when no halal exists, there is no sin on the one who does not know. Fast food chains differ by country and branch: check the certificate. The Masajid and Halal screen can help you find halal places.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 5:3, 5:5.\n[GO:/masajid]',"halal meat",!0,"")
B.aW0=s(["\\btattoo\\w*\\b","\\bpiercing\\w*\\b","\\bear ?piercing\\w*\\b","\\bnose ?piercing\\w*\\b","\\bbody (?:art|modification)\\b","\\bpermanent makeup\\b","\\bmicroblading\\b","\\bhenna\\b.*\\b(?:halal|haram|allowed|sunnah)\\b"],t.s)
B.aY9=s(["\\b(?:wudu|ghusl|prayer)\\b"],t.s)
B.amd=new A.aN("tattoo",B.aW0,B.aY9,"Permanent tattoos are forbidden according to most scholars. The Prophet \ufdfa said that Allah cursed the woman who tattoos and the one who has it done (Sahih al-Bukhari 5937). The reasons scholars give are that it changes Allah's creation, causes pain and harm, and blocks water in some views.\n\nPractical points:\n- If you got a tattoo before you were a Muslim, or before you knew, there is no sin on you now; repent and go forward. Wudu and prayer are valid with a tattoo.\n- Removing it: scholars say you are not required to remove a tattoo if removal would cause serious harm or great pain; remove it if that is easily done.\n- Henna and other temporary designs that wash off are allowed (for women and in moderation). Medical tattoos (for example, marking a radiation site or hiding a scar) are allowed by necessity.\n- Ear piercing for women is allowed; most scholars dislike or forbid body piercing for men that imitates women, and extreme body modification.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 5937.","tattoo",!0,"")
B.b6U=s(["\\bdogs?\\b","\\bpuppy\\b","\\bpuppies\\b","\\bguide dogs?\\b","\\bservice dogs?\\b","\\bhunting dogs?\\b","\\bsniffer\\b"],t.s)
B.bcL=s(["\\b(?:najis|najasa|impure)\\b","\\b(?:game|story|stories)\\b","\\bcats?\\b"],t.s)
B.am0=new A.aN("dogs",B.b6U,B.bcL,"Dogs: scholars differ on keeping and touching them.\n- Keeping a dog as a house pet is discouraged: the Prophet \ufdfa said that whoever keeps a dog that is neither a watch dog nor a hunting dog loses two qirat of his good deeds every day (Sahih al-Bukhari 5480). Keeping a dog for hunting, herding or guarding is allowed, and most scholars add a guide or service dog for a person who needs it.\n- Touching a dog or its saliva: the Shafi'i and Hanbali schools treat the saliva as impure and require washing seven times; the Maliki school treats the dog as pure; see the impurity answer. Dry contact is not a problem in any school.\n- The angels: the Prophet \ufdfa said the angels of mercy do not enter a house where there is a dog or a picture of a living creature (Sahih al-Bukhari 3225). Scholars explain this in different ways.\n\nWhatever you decide, be kind to dogs: cruelty to any animal is a sin, and feeding or giving water to a thirsty one is rewarded.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 5480, 3225.","pets",!0,"")
B.aV2=s(["\\bpets?\\b","\\bcats?\\b","\\bkitten\\w*\\b","\\bkeep(?:ing)? (?:a )?(?:bird|birds|fish|rabbit|animals?|hamster|parrot|turtle|horse)\\b","\\banimals?\\b.*\\b(?:rights|treat\\w*|cruel\\w*|kind\\w*)\\b","\\bcruelty to animals\\b","\\btreat\\w* animals\\b","\\banimal rights\\b"],t.s)
B.b_A=s(["\\b(?:game|story|stories)\\b","\\b(?:najis|najasa|impure)\\b","\\b(?:sacrifice|qurbani|udhiyah|aqiqah)\\b","\\bdogs?\\b"],t.s)
B.aln=new A.aN("pets-animals",B.aV2,B.b_A,"Cats: yes, you may keep them. The Prophet \ufdfa said of the cat that it is not unclean (Sunan Abu Dawud 75, hasan sahih per al-Albani). He also warned that a woman entered the Fire because of a cat she had tied up and neither fed nor let go (Sahih al-Bukhari 3318). So caring for any animal is rewarded, and cruelty to one is a sin.\n\nOther pets (birds, fish, rabbits and so on) are allowed if you feed them, give them room and do not harm them. Do not keep a bird in a cage so small that it cannot live well. For dogs, ask me about dogs.\n\nSource: Sunan Abu Dawud 75, hasan sahih per al-Albani; Sahih al-Bukhari 3318.","pets",!0,"")
B.bml=s(["\\bgold\\b.*\\b(?:men|man|male|males|boys?|husband|him)\\b","\\b(?:men|man|male|males|boys?)\\b.*\\bgold\\b","\\bsilk\\b.*\\b(?:men|man|male|males)\\b","\\b(?:men|man|male|males)\\b.*\\bsilk\\b","\\bgold (?:ring|chain|watch|necklace|jewel\\w*)\\b","\\bsilver ring\\b","\\bwear(?:ing)? (?:gold|silk|platinum|diamonds?|jewel\\w*)\\b","\\bplatinum\\b"],t.s)
B.b86=s(["\\b(?:zakat|nisab|sell|buy|invest|price|crypto)\\b","\\bgold standard\\b"],t.s)
B.am4=new A.aN("gold-silk-men",B.bml,B.b86,"For men, wearing gold and pure silk is forbidden; for women both are allowed. Abu Musa reported the Messenger of Allah said wearing silk and gold has been made unlawful for the males of my Ummah and lawful for its females (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 1720, sahih per Darussalam). He once pulled a gold ring from a man's hand and threw it away (Sahih Muslim 2090).\n\nSilver is allowed for men, and a silver ring was worn by the Prophet \ufdfa (Sahih al-Bukhari 5865). Other metals such as platinum or steel, and silk mixed with other cloth, are allowed by most scholars; ask a scholar if you are unsure. A gold tooth filling or a gold nose or surgery fixture for medical need is allowed.\n\nSource: Jami' at-Tirmidhi 1720, sahih per Darussalam; Sahih Muslim 2090; Sahih al-Bukhari 5865.","gold",!0,"")
B.b2D=s(["\\bdy(?:e|ing) (?:my |the )?hair\\b","\\bhair dye\\b","\\bhair colou?r\\w*\\b","\\bcolou?r(?:ing)? (?:my |the )?hair\\b","\\bblack dye\\b","\\bgrey hair\\b","\\bgray hair\\b","\\bwhite hair\\b","\\bhair extensions?\\b","\\bwigs?\\b","\\bbleach(?:ing)? (?:my |the )?hair\\b","\\bhighlights\\b.*\\bhair\\b","\\bcut(?:ting)? (?:my |the )?hair\\b","\\bhaircut\\b","\\bqaza\\b","\\bshav\\w* (?:my )?(?:head|legs?|armpits?|pubic)\\b","\\bfitrah\\b.*\\b(?:nails?|hair)\\b"],t.s)
B.alc=new A.aN("hair-dye",B.b2D,B.b,"Hair colour:\n- Grey or white hair: the Prophet \ufdfa told Abu Bakr's father, whose hair and beard were white, to change the colour with something but to avoid black (Sahih Muslim 2102). So henna, or a brown or yellow dye, is recommended; black is disliked by most scholars (some scholars allow it for women, or in war).\n- Dyeing hair that is not grey for fashion, in a non-black colour, is allowed for both men and women by most scholars, as long as the dye is not a barrier that stops water from reaching the hair (it must be washable-through in wudu: stains are fine; paint-like coatings are not).\n- Bleaching or highlights: allowed, within modesty, as long as you are not imitating a way of life that Islam forbids.\n\nHair extensions: attaching other people's hair is forbidden in the hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari 5937; most scholars apply it to real human hair, and some extend it to anything attached that looks like your own. Wigs: scholars differ.\n\nCutting hair: allowed for men and women, with modesty. A woman may trim her hair; she should not cut it to imitate men.\n\nBody hair: removing pubic and underarm hair, clipping the nails and trimming the moustache are part of the fitrah, the natural practices (Sahih Muslim 258). Removing hair elsewhere, such as legs or arms, is not forbidden for a woman; men should not imitate women in ways that are specific to them. Shaving the whole head is allowed for a man, and is part of the rites of Hajj and Umrah.\n\nSource: Sahih Muslim 2102, 258; Sahih al-Bukhari 5937.","hair",!0,"")
B.aJp=s(["\\bbirthdays?\\b","\\bcelebrat\\w*\\b.*\\b(?:christmas|easter|halloween|diwali|new year|new years|valentine\\w*|thanksgiving|mothers day|fathers day|anniversary|holi|lunar new year|chinese new year|hanukkah|st patrick\\w*|april fool\\w*|mawlid|milad|prophets birthday|shab e barat|nisf shaban)\\b","\\b(?:christmas|easter|halloween|diwali|new year|new years|valentine\\w*|thanksgiving|mothers day|fathers day|holi|lunar new year|chinese new year|hanukkah|mawlid|milad|prophets birthday|shab e barat|nisf shaban)\\b.*\\b(?:muslims?|islam|haram|halal|allowed|permissible|celebrate|greet|wish)\\b","\\bmerry christmas\\b","\\bhappy (?:new year|diwali|christmas|easter|birthday|holidays?)\\b","\\bwish(?:ing)? (?:my |a )?(?:non muslim|christian|friend|neighbou?r|colleague)\\w*\\b.*\\b(?:christmas|easter|diwali|holiday)\\b","\\bcan muslims? celebrate\\b","\\bis it (?:haram|allowed|ok|okay) to celebrate\\b","\\bgift\\w* (?:to|for) (?:a )?non muslim\\b","\\bnon muslim (?:holiday|festival|celebration)\\w*\\b"],t.s)
B.amJ=new A.aN("birthday-festivals",B.aJp,B.b,"Islam has two festivals for the community: Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha. The Prophet \ufdfa said there is an Eid for every people and this is ours (Sahih al-Bukhari 952).\n\nBirthdays: scholars differ. Many scholars say that marking a birthday as a regular celebration is not from the practice of the Prophet \ufdfa and his companions, and so avoid it, and some of them consider it an imitation of others. Other scholars see a birthday party or a gift as a social custom, not worship, and so permitted, as long as it has nothing forbidden in it and is not treated as a religious ritual. Being kind to people on their day, thanking Allah for your life, giving thanks and making du'a is good on any day. Whichever view you follow, do not make it a matter for quarrelling in the family.\n\nFestivals of other faiths (Christmas, Diwali, Easter and so on): most scholars say a Muslim should not take part in the religious rituals or the religious side of another faith's festival, nor give gifts that mark it as worship. Allah also asks us to be good and just to neighbours and co-workers of other faiths: \"Allah does not forbid you from those who do not fight you because of religion and do not expel you from your homes - from being righteous toward them and acting justly toward them\" (Qur'an 60:8). Many scholars therefore allow a polite greeting or a card in return for theirs, and attending a family or workplace event without joining in the ritual; others are stricter and prefer not to congratulate on a religious day. Both are held by scholars.\n\nMawlid (the Prophet's birthday), Shab-e-Barat and similar nights: scholars differ widely, some treating them as good remembrance, others as additions to the religion. I will not rule between them; follow a scholar you trust and avoid anything forbidden.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 952; Qur'an 60:8.","celebrations",!0,"")
B.aUy=s(["\\bvot(?:e|ing|ed)\\b","\\belections?\\b","\\bpolitics\\b","\\bpolitical part\\w*\\b","\\bdemocracy\\b","\\bdemocratic\\b","\\bparticipat\\w* in (?:politics|government|elections?)\\b","\\bwork(?:ing)? (?:for|in) (?:the )?(?:government|police|army|military)\\b"],t.s)
B.blO=s(["\\b(?:vote for (?:me|my)|game|poll in (?:the )?app)\\b"],t.s)
B.amQ=new A.aN("voting-politics",B.aUy,B.blO,"Scholars differ on voting and political participation in non-Muslim-majority countries and under man-made systems:\n- Many contemporary scholars, and fiqh councils for Muslim minorities, say voting is allowed and even encouraged where it helps the community, reduces harm, protects Muslims' rights, or brings a better candidate. They argue from the principle of weighing benefit and harm, and from the Prophet's own treaties and just dealings.\n- Some scholars say it is not allowed, because it involves taking part in a system that is not built on Islamic law, or because the candidates support what is forbidden.\n- Between these, some say it depends on the circumstances: vote for the lesser harm, or for a candidate who defends justice, when not voting would bring greater harm.\n\nAllah commands justice and honesty in trusts and in rule; Muslims in any country are told to be good citizens who obey the law as long as it does not command disobedience to Allah, and to be just to all. This app takes no side in politics and does not tell you whom to vote for. Ask a local scholar who knows the situation where you live.","voting",!0,"")
B.b5b=s(["\\bmusic\\b","\\bmusical instruments?\\b","\\bsinging\\b","\\bsongs?\\b","\\bnasheeds?\\b","\\banasheed\\b","\\bduff\\b","\\bdrums?\\b","\\bguitar\\b","\\bpiano\\b","\\bconcerts?\\b","\\bflute\\b","\\bdancing\\b","\\blistening to (?:music|songs?|the radio)\\b","\\bis (?:music|singing) (?:haram|halal|allowed)\\b"],t.s)
B.bgO=s(["\\b(?:player|play (?:a|the|some)|app|screen|where|find|open|queue|download)\\b"],t.s)
B.akY=new A.aN("music",B.b5b,B.bgO,"Scholars have two positions on music, and both rest on evidence:\n- Most classical scholars across the schools, and many today, say musical instruments (other than the simple hand drum, the duff, at weddings and Eid) are forbidden. They rely on the report that some of the Prophet's followers will treat as lawful fornication, silk, wine and musical instruments (Sahih al-Bukhari 5590). Other scholars question the chain of this report, because Bukhari gives it without a full chain.\n- Other scholars, including some classical ones such as Ibn Hazm, and many modern ones, say no clearly authentic text forbids music itself, and that it is allowed when the words and setting are free of what is forbidden.\n\nBoth agree that the duff and singing are allowed at weddings and Eid. The Prophet \ufdfa defended the girls singing in A'ishah's house, saying there is an Eid for every people (Sahih al-Bukhari 952).\n\nNasheeds with only the human voice, or with a duff, are accepted by nearly all scholars if the words are clean. Songs about love affairs, drink or immorality are discouraged by both views. This is a real difference, so follow the scholar you trust and avoid anything that distracts you from prayer and the Qur'an. For your own situation, ask a qualified scholar.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 5590, 952.","music",!0,"")
B.bnr=s(["\\bbeards?\\b","\\bshav\\w* (?:my )?(?:face|beard)\\b","\\btrim\\w* (?:my )?beard\\b","\\bmoustache\\b","\\bmustache\\b","\\bclean shaven\\b","\\bshaving\\b","\\bshave\\b"],t.s)
B.ank=new A.aN("beard",B.bnr,B.b,"The Prophet \ufdfa said: trim the moustache and let the beard grow (Sahih al-Bukhari 5892).\n\nThe scholars' positions:\n- Most scholars (Hanafi, Maliki, Hanbali, and many Shafi'i) say growing the beard is required and shaving is forbidden.\n- Some Shafi'i scholars and many modern scholars say it is a strongly recommended sunnah and that shaving is disliked, not a major sin.\n- Trimming the beard: many allow trimming what extends beyond a fist's length; some say leave it entirely.\n\nIf you cannot grow a full beard, or you are in a job that insists on shaving, ask a scholar about your situation. A beard is not what makes a person a good Muslim; character, prayer and honesty come first.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 5892.","beard",!0,"")
B.bdC=s(["\\bhijab\\b","\\bniqab\\b","\\bburqa\\b","\\bheadscarf\\b","\\bhead scarf\\b","\\bveil\\w*\\b","\\bcover(?:ing)? (?:my |her |the )?hair\\b","\\bwhy (?:do )?(?:muslim )?(?:women|girls) (?:wear|cover)\\b","\\bmodest(?:y)? dress\\b","\\bawrah\\b","\\bwhat (?:should|must) (?:a )?(?:woman|women|man|men) (?:wear|cover)\\b","\\bdress code\\b"],t.s)
B.A0=s(["\\b(?:game|story)\\b"],t.s)
B.akQ=new A.aN("hijab",B.bdC,B.A0,"Allah tells believing women: \"And tell the believing women to reduce [some] of their vision and guard their private parts and not expose their adornment except that which [necessarily] appears thereof and to wrap [a portion of] their headcovers over their chests\" (Qur'an 24:31). And in Qur'an 33:59 He tells the Prophet to say to his wives, daughters and the believing women to bring down over themselves some of their outer garments.\n\nThe scholars agree that a woman covers her body in front of men who are not close relatives, and that men also have to cover from navel to knee and lower their gaze (Qur'an 24:30). Most say the woman's face and hands may be uncovered, while some say the face too is covered (niqab); both views have scholars behind them. The hijab is a command in Islam, and a woman who is working up to it is still on the path.\n\nHijab is more than cloth: it is also modesty in behaviour, for men and women alike. Nobody should be forced into it or mocked for it; Allah says \"There shall be no compulsion in [acceptance of] the religion\" (Qur'an 2:256).\n\nSource: Qur'an 24:31, 33:59, 24:30, 2:256.","hijab",!0,"")
B.b3b=s(["\\bsmok\\w*\\b","\\bcigarettes?\\b","\\btobacco\\b","\\bvap(?:e|ing|es)\\b","\\bshisha\\b","\\bhookah\\b","\\bcannabis\\b","\\bmarijuana\\b","\\bweed\\b","\\bdrugs?\\b","\\bcocaine\\b","\\bkhat\\b","\\bnicotine\\b"],t.s)
B.aYN=s(["\\b(?:fasting|fast)\\b.*\\b(?:break|breaks|invalid\\w*)\\b"],t.s)
B.alI=new A.aN("smoking-vaping",B.b3b,B.aYN,'Smoking and vaping: most scholars today say they are forbidden, because they harm the body and Allah says "do not throw [yourselves] with your [own] hands into destruction" (Qur\'an 2:195) and "makes lawful for them the good things and prohibits for them the evil" (Qur\'an 7:157). Some older scholars called it only disliked, before the harm was known. Shisha and hookah are treated the same.\n\nDrugs that intoxicate, such as cannabis, cocaine and similar, are forbidden for the same reason as alcohol: see the answer on alcohol.\n\nIf you are trying to quit, that is a good intention and Allah rewards the effort. Ask a doctor for help, and remember that it breaks the fast (see the fasting answer).\n\nSource: Qur\'an 2:195, 7:157.',"smoking",!0,"")
B.b_k=s(["\\bastrolog\\w*\\b","\\bhoroscopes?\\b","\\bzodiac\\b","\\bfortune ?tell\\w*\\b","\\bpalm ?read\\w*\\b","\\btarot\\b","\\bsuperstiti\\w*\\b","\\bfriday (?:the )?13(?:th)?\\b","\\bbad luck\\b","\\bevil eye\\b","\\bblack cat\\b","\\blucky (?:number|charm)s?\\b","\\bmagic\\b","\\bsihr\\b","\\bwitchcraft\\b","\\bamulet\\w*\\b","\\btaweez\\b","\\btalisman\\w*\\b","\\bjinn possess\\w*\\b"],t.s)
B.an0=new A.aN("superstition-astrology",B.b_k,B.A0,"Islam rejects fortune-telling, astrology, horoscopes and omens of bad luck. Only Allah knows the unseen: \"None in the heavens and earth knows the unseen except Allah\" (Qur'an 27:65). The Prophet \ufdfa also said that whoever goes to a fortune-teller and asks him about anything, his prayer will not be accepted for forty nights (Sahih Muslim 2230). Believing in the diviner is worse.\n\nSo: do not read your horoscope as guidance, do not treat Friday the 13th, a black cat or a number as bad or good luck, and do not wear charms to bring luck or ward off harm; put your trust in Allah. Black magic (sihr) is real in the Qur'an but doing it is a major sin, and you protect yourself with the Qur'an and the morning and evening remembrances.\n\nThe evil eye is also real, and the cure is the Qur'anic protections and du'a, not amulets or tying things. If you are worried about harm to you, say Ayat al-Kursi and the last three surahs.\n\nSource: Qur'an 27:65; Sahih Muslim 2230.\n[GO:/adhkar]","superstition",!0,"")
B.aTO=s(["\\b(?:chess|ludo|backgammon|nardshir|nardashir|dice|dominoes?|playing cards?|card games?|poker|board games?|video games?|gaming|gamers?|playstation|xbox|pubg|fortnite|minecraft|roblox|esports|e sports|monopoly|carrom)\\b.*\\b(?:haram|halal|allowed|permissible|permitted|forbidden|ruling|sin|sinful|islamic|islam|muslims?|makruh)\\b","\\b(?:haram|halal|allowed|permissible|permitted|forbidden|ruling|sin|sinful|makruh)\\b.*\\b(?:chess|ludo|backgammon|dice|dominoes?|playing cards?|card games?|poker|board games?|video games?|gaming|playstation|xbox|pubg|fortnite|minecraft|roblox|esports|monopoly|carrom)\\b","\\b(?:can|may|should|do) (?:muslims?|i|we) (?:play|playing) (?:chess|ludo|backgammon|cards|games|video games|dice|board games|football|sports)\\b","\\bis (?:playing|it (?:haram|halal|ok|okay|allowed) to play) (?:chess|ludo|backgammon|cards|games|video games|dice|board games)\\b"],t.s)
B.b63=s(["\\b(?:how (?:do|to) (?:i )?play|rules|where is|in this app|in the app)\\b"],t.s)
B.anc=new A.aN("games-ruling",B.aTO,B.b63,"Scholars have different views here, and it depends on the game.\n\n- Gambling: any game played for money or a stake is gambling and forbidden (Qur'an 5:90, and see the answer on gambling). That includes card games with stakes, and loot boxes or paid chances in video games that work like a lottery.\n- Backgammon and dice games: the Prophet \ufdfa condemned backgammon in strong words (Sahih Muslim 2260). Many classical scholars apply this to games decided by dice, such as Ludo; others say the report is about backgammon and gambling and does not cover every dice game. Scholars differ on this.\n- Chess: the schools differed. The Shafi'i school held it to be disliked rather than forbidden; the Hanafi, Maliki and Hanbali schools held it to be forbidden or strongly disliked, especially when it takes the place of prayer or is played for stakes. Many scholars today allow it as a mental exercise when there is no betting, it does not make you miss prayers or your duties, and it is not played to excess. Please follow the scholar you trust.\n- Video games and other games: the game itself is generally allowed when its content is lawful (no immodesty, no gambling, no mockery of religion), and when it does not take you from prayer, family and work. Allah says that Satan wants to turn people from the remembrance of Allah and from prayer through intoxicants and gambling (Qur'an 5:91), so watch the time you spend.\n\nIn this app, the games are made to teach and have no betting, coins, ratings or purchases.\n\nSource: Qur'an 5:90, 5:91; Sahih Muslim 2260.\n[GO:/games]","games ruling",!0,"")
B.aXP=s(["\\bmy (?:husband|wife|spouse|partner|father|dad|mother|mom|brother|in laws?|fiance\\w*|boyfriend|girlfriend) (?:hits|beats|beat|hit|slaps|slapped|abuses|abused|hurts|hurt|is (?:violent|abusive)|threatens|chokes|punches)\\b","\\b(?:i am|i m|im) (?:being )?(?:abused|beaten|hit|hurt by my)\\b","\\babusive (?:marriage|husband|wife|home|relationship|parents?|father)\\b","\\b(?:afraid|scared|terrified) of my (?:husband|wife|father|dad|partner)\\b","\\b(?:husband|wife) (?:beats|hits|abuses) me\\b","\\bdomestic (?:violence|abuse)\\b.*\\b(?:me|my|i)\\b","\\bi (?:am|m) a victim of (?:domestic|abuse)\\b"],t.s)
B.akX=new A.aN("abused-spouse",B.aXP,B.b,"I am sorry. What is happening to you is not your fault, and Islam does not ask you to bear violence.\n\nFirst, your safety: if you are in danger right now, go to a safe place and call your local emergency number. Tell someone you trust (a relative, a friend, a doctor), and keep a record of injuries and messages. A local domestic-violence service, which you can find through your doctor or an online search for your country, can help in secret.\n\nIslam gives you rights: Allah commands living with a spouse \"in kindness\" (Qur'an 4:19), the Prophet \ufdfa never struck a woman or a servant with his hand (Sahih Muslim 2328), and when a husband broke his wife's arm, the Prophet \ufdfa ordered that she be set free from him (Sunan an-Nasa'i 3497, sahih per al-Albani). A wife can ask for separation by khul' or through a court, and a girl or woman does not need to stay with someone who harms her.\n\nAn imam or scholar can support you, but you do not have to ask permission to protect yourself, and you should not be asked to keep quiet. If you want, you can also ask me about khul' and divorce in general.\n\nSource: Qur'an 4:19; Sahih Muslim 2328; Sunan an-Nasa'i 3497, sahih per al-Albani.","abuse",!0,"")
B.b3o=s(["\\b(?:hit|hits|hitting|beat|beats|beating|slap|slaps|slapping|strike|striking|smack|spank|discipline|disciplining|punish|punishing|hurt|hurting)\\b.*\\b(?:wife|wives|women|woman|spouse|girlfriend)\\b","\\b(?:wife|wives|women|woman)\\b.*\\b(?:hit|hits|hitting|beat|beaten|beating|slap|strike|striking|struck)\\b","\\bhusband\\b.*\\b(?:allowed|permitted|right|permission)\\b.*\\b(?:hit|beat|strike|discipline)\\b","\\b4 ?:? ?34\\b","\\bnushuz\\b","\\bwife beating\\b","\\bbeat(?:ing)? (?:their|your|his|a) wi(?:fe|ves)\\b","\\bdomestic (?:violence|abuse)\\b","\\bmen are in charge of women\\b"],t.s)
B.aSk=s(["\\b(?:my (?:husband|father|dad|brother|in laws?) (?:hits|beats))\\b"],t.s)
B.alK=new A.aN("hitting-wife",B.b3o,B.aSk,"No. Hitting a wife in anger, to control her or to make her afraid, is wrong in Islam, and a man who does it is answerable to Allah for it. The Prophet \ufdfa never did it. A'isha reported that the Prophet never beat anyone with his hand, neither a woman nor a servant, except when fighting in the cause of Allah (Sahih Muslim 2328). He also said: None of you should flog his wife as he flogs a slave and then have sexual intercourse with her in the last part of the day (Sahih al-Bukhari 5204).\n\nThe verse people quote is \"But those [wives] from whom you fear arrogance - [first] advise them; [then if they persist], forsake them in bed; and [finally], strike them. But if they obey you [once more], seek no means against them\" (Qur'an 4:34). It is about a husband facing serious, persistent misconduct, it comes last after advice and separation in bed, and the scholars restrict it heavily:\n- In his farewell sermon the Prophet \ufdfa allowed only a beating that does not harm (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 1163, hasan per al-Albani).\n- Never on the face, and never insulting her (Sunan Abu Dawud 2142, hasan sahih per al-Albani).\n- Many scholars say that at most it is symbolic, such as a touch with a folded cloth or a siwak, that causes no pain, bruise or mark and no humiliation, and that leaving it is better. In today's world it should not be used, and any hurt or injury is a sin and a crime.\n- When many women went to his family to complain about their husbands, he said those men are not the best of you (Sunan Ibn Majah 1985, sahih per Darussalam).\n\nThe right course in a marital problem is to speak kindly, make peace, and, if needed, bring two fair relatives (Qur'an 4:35). Allah describes marriage as \"He placed between you affection and mercy\" (Qur'an 30:21), and Abu Hurairah narrated the Prophet said the most complete of the believers in faith is the one with the best character, and the best of you are those who are best to your women (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 1162, hasan sahih per al-Albani).\n\nIf you are being hit, or you are afraid for your safety, ask me about abuse and I will tell you what you can do.\n\nSource: Sahih Muslim 2328; Sahih al-Bukhari 5204; Qur'an 4:34, 4:35, 30:21; Jami' at-Tirmidhi 1163, hasan per al-Albani; Sunan Abu Dawud 2142, hasan sahih per al-Albani; Sunan Ibn Majah 1985, sahih per Darussalam; Jami' at-Tirmidhi 1162, hasan sahih per al-Albani.","wife",!0,"")
B.bbF=s(["\\bnikah\\b","\\bnikkah\\b","\\bmahr\\b","\\bmehr\\b","\\bdowry\\b","\\bbridal gift\\b","\\bwali\\b","\\bguardian\\b.*\\b(?:marriage|marry|nikah)\\b","\\b(?:conditions|requirements|pillars) of (?:a )?(?:marriage|nikah)\\b","\\bhow (?:do|to|can) (?:i |we )?(?:get )?married\\b","\\bmarriage contract\\b","\\bforced marriage\\b","\\barranged marriage\\b","\\bmarry without (?:my )?(?:parents?|family|wali|father)\\b","\\bwalima\\b","\\bis (?:marriage|nikah) (?:compulsory|obligatory|fard|sunnah|required|mandatory)\\b","\\bwhy (?:should|do) (?:i|muslims?) (?:get )?marr(?:y|ied)\\b","^marriage$","\\bhow to marry\\b","\\bwhat is marriage\\b","\\bislamic wedding\\b","\\bengagement\\b"],t.s)
B.b_O=s(["\\b(?:games?|story|prophets marriage|marriages of the prophet)\\b","\\b(?:non muslim|christian|jewish|jew|hindu|buddhist|atheist)\\b"],t.s)
B.am6=new A.aN("nikah-basics",B.bbF,B.b_O,"Marriage (nikah) is a sunnah the Prophet \ufdfa encouraged, and he forbade giving up marriage for worship (Sunan Ibn Majah 1849, sahih per Darussalam). It is a strong covenant: Allah calls it a solemn covenant (Qur'an 4:21), and \"He placed between you affection and mercy\" (Qur'an 30:21).\n\nWhat makes a nikah valid (the schools differ in detail):\n- Consent of both. A woman must not be married against her will: The Prophet said a matron should not be given in marriage without consulting her, and a virgin not without her permission, which is her silence (Sahih al-Bukhari 5136). A forced marriage is wrong.\n- A guardian (wali) for the woman: Abu Musa narrated the Messenger of Allah said there is no marriage except with a wali (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 1101, sahih per Darussalam). The Hanafi school allows a mature woman to contract her own marriage with the same conditions of suitability; the other schools require the wali.\n- Mahr (bridal gift) given to the wife, which belongs to her: \"And give the women [upon marriage] their [bridal] gifts graciously\" (Qur'an 4:4). Its amount is by agreement and does not have to be large.\n- Two witnesses and an open announcement (as most scholars require), and an offer and acceptance in clear words. A feast (walima) follows.\n\nSeek a person of faith and character; the Prophet \ufdfa said that a woman is married for wealth, family, beauty and religion, so choose the one with religion (Sahih al-Bukhari 5090).\n\nA couple living together without a valid nikah is not married, whatever the civil paper says, and a nikah without registration may be a problem for your rights in your country, so many scholars advise doing both.\n\nSource: Sunan Ibn Majah 1849, sahih per Darussalam; Qur'an 4:21, 30:21, 4:4; Sahih al-Bukhari 5136, 5090; Jami' at-Tirmidhi 1101, sahih per Darussalam.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-nikah]\n[GO:/academy/family-life]","marriage",!0,"")
B.b8h=s(["\\bwhat (?:is|s|does) (?:a |the )?khul\\w*\\b","\\bkhul\\w* (?:meaning|means|definition|mean)\\b","\\b(?:meaning|definition) of khul\\w*\\b","^(?:explain )?khul\\w*\\b","\\bkhul\\w* (?:vs|versus|or|and) (?:talaq|divorce|faskh)\\b","\\b(?:talaq|divorce|faskh) (?:vs|versus|or|and) khul\\w*\\b","\\bdifference between (?:talaq|divorce|faskh)\\b.*\\bkhul\\w*\\b","\\bdifference between khul\\w*\\b","^khul\\w*$"],t.s)
B.amI=new A.aN("khula-what",B.b8h,B.b,"Khul' is when a wife asks to be released from the marriage and gives something back, usually the mahr or what the couple agree on, and the husband accepts. It is the wife's own way of ending a marriage, as talaq is the husband's. The word means \"taking off\", as if she takes the marriage off.\n\nWhere it comes from:\n- The Qur'an allows it when the couple fear they cannot keep within Allah's limits: \"there is no blame upon either of them concerning that by which she ransoms herself\" (Qur'an 2:229).\n- The Prophet \ufdfa applied it for the wife of Thabit ibn Qais: she said she had no complaint about his character or religion, but she did not want to live in a way that went against Islam if she stayed. He asked her to give back the garden she had received, and told Thabit to take it and release her (Sahih al-Bukhari 5273).\n\nHow it differs from the other ways a marriage ends:\n- Talaq: the husband pronounces the divorce, and he owes the wife her mahr.\n- Khul': the wife asks, gives something back, and the husband agrees. It ends the marriage in one step, and is counted as a single irrevocable separation by most scholars.\n- A judge's decree: when a husband harms his wife or refuses what he owes her, a judge or a recognised Islamic court can end the marriage even if he does not agree. The schools differ on the grounds.\n\nAsk me \"can a woman ask for khula\" for who decides, what she gives back and the waiting period. This is general information, not a ruling on your case.\n\nSource: Qur'an 2:229; Sahih al-Bukhari 5273.\n[GO:/huda]","divorce",!1,"")
B.bkY=s(["\\bkhul\\w*\\b"],t.s)
B.al2=new A.aN("khula-woman",B.bkY,B.b,"A wife can ask for khul' whether or not there is a fault in her husband. The Prophet's ruling in the case of Thabit ibn Qais's wife did not ask her to prove mistreatment; it was enough that she could not stay in the marriage as she was. But she should not ask lightly: Thawban reported that the Prophet said if a woman asks her husband for khul' without any harm or cause, the scent of Paradise will be unlawful for her (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 1187, sahih per Darussalam). (Al-Tirmidhi called it hasan.) A cause can be harm, unkindness, or a deep dislike that makes living together a sin.\n\nHow it usually goes:\n- She tells her husband, or the family who help them, that she wants khul', and what she is ready to return. The usual amount is the mahr she received, and not more: the Prophet asked only for the garden back.\n- If he agrees, the khul' is made, preferably in front of witnesses and with a record, because a civil divorce is a separate step in most countries.\n- If he refuses, the schools and the courts differ. Many scholars, and the family courts or Islamic councils of many Muslim countries, allow a judge to dissolve the marriage, with or without his consent, when the wife shows a good reason. Ask a scholar of your school, and a lawyer, how it works where you live.\n- Her waiting period (iddah) follows. The Prophet ordered one menstrual cycle for a woman who had made khul' (Sunan Abu Dawud 2229, sahih per al-Albani). Many scholars hold to the full three cycles; the schools differ.\n\nIf your husband is hurting you or you are afraid, tell me, and I will answer that first. This is general information, not a ruling on your own case.\n\nSource: Jami' at-Tirmidhi 1187, sahih per Darussalam; Sunan Abu Dawud 2229, sahih per al-Albani.\n[GO:/huda]","divorce",!1,"")
B.aIr=s(["\\bdivorce\\b","\\btalaq\\b","\\btalaaq\\b","\\bkhula\\w*\\b","\\biddah\\b","\\biddat\\b","\\bwaiting period\\b.*\\b(?:divorce|widow)\\b","\\bseparat\\w*\\b.*\\b(?:husband|wife|marriage)\\b","\\bwhat is islams view on divorce\\b"],t.s)
B.ani=new A.aN("divorce-overview",B.aIr,B.b,"Divorce is allowed in Islam as a last resort, but it is the most disliked of the permitted things in the view of Allah: this is reported in a hadith, which some scholars grade weak, so I do not rely on it. What is firmly established is the Qur'an's guidance: \"Divorce is twice. Then, either keep [her] in an acceptable manner or release [her] with good treatment\" (Qur'an 2:229), and \"retain them according to acceptable terms or part with them according to acceptable terms\" (Qur'an 65:2).\n\nHow it works, in outline:\n- Before divorce, try to settle: speak, bring a fair relative from each side (Qur'an 4:35), or make a settlement (Qur'an 4:128).\n- Talaq is pronounced by the husband. The wife waits a set period (iddah) so that they may reconcile, and he must not turn her out of the house during it (Qur'an 65:1).\n- The wife can also seek separation: khul' (she returns the dower and is released), as the Prophet \ufdfa did in the case of Thabit ibn Qais's wife (Sahih al-Bukhari 5273), or through a court.\n- A wife should not ask for divorce without a good reason: Thawban reported the Prophet said if any woman asks her husband for divorce without a strong reason, the odour of Paradise will be forbidden to her (Sunan Abu Dawud 2226, sahih per al-Albani).\n- Children and money matters: the rights of the wife and children stay, including maintenance and kind treatment.\n\nThe details are where the schools and the laws of each country differ, in particular on repeated or written talaq, so I cannot say whether a divorce has taken place in your case. Please ask a qualified scholar or Islamic court with the exact words and circumstances.\n\nSource: Qur'an 2:229, 65:2, 4:35, 4:128, 65:1; Sahih al-Bukhari 5273; Sunan Abu Dawud 2226, sahih per al-Albani.","divorce",!1,"")
B.aZL=s(["\\b(?:marry|marrying|marriage|married|wed|date|dating)\\b.*\\b(?:non muslim|christian|jewish|jew|hindu|buddhist|sikh|atheist|kafir|disbeliever|people of the book|ahl al kitab)\\b","\\b(?:non muslim|christian|jewish|jew|hindu|buddhist|sikh|atheist|kafir|disbeliever)\\b.*\\b(?:marry|marrying|marriage|husband|wife|spouse)\\b","\\binterfaith\\b","\\binter faith\\b","\\bmixed marriage\\b","\\bmarry outside (?:islam|the faith)\\b"],t.s)
B.alF=new A.aN("interfaith-marriage",B.aZL,B.b,"The rulings differ for a man and a woman:\n- A Muslim woman may not marry a non-Muslim man. This is agreed among scholars, based on Qur'an 2:221 and \"they are not lawful [wives] for them, nor are they lawful [husbands] for them\" (Qur'an 60:10). He may marry her if he accepts Islam.\n- A Muslim man may marry a chaste woman from the People of the Book (Jews and Christians): \"And [lawful in marriage are] chaste women from among the believers and chaste women from among those who were given the Scripture before you, when you have given them their due compensation\" (Qur'an 5:5). Most scholars allow it, with the warning that the children should be raised on Islam and that it is preferable to marry a Muslim. Some modern scholars discourage it where it harms the family's faith. Marriage with a woman who follows no scripture (an atheist, or most other religions) is not allowed until she becomes Muslim; scholars differ on some groups.\n\nAllah also said: \"And do not marry polytheistic women until they believe\" (Qur'an 2:221). Practical advice: raise this early, and ask a scholar who knows your law and circumstances.\n\nSource: Qur'an 2:221, 60:10, 5:5.","interfaith",!0,"")
B.aH0=s(["\\bboy ?friends?\\b","\\bgirl ?friends?\\b","\\bdating\\b","\\bdate (?:a|someone|my|him|her)\\b","\\bpre ?marital\\b","\\bpremarital\\b","\\bcourtship\\b","\\b(?:talk|text|chat|speak|message|go out)\\w* (?:to|with) (?:a )?(?:girl|girls|boy|boys|guy|guys|opposite gender|opposite sex|non mahram|him|her)\\b","\\bhalal (?:dating|relationship|courtship)\\b","\\bharam relationship\\b","\\b(?:kiss|kissing|hug|hugging|touch|touching|holding hands)\\b.*\\b(?:before marriage|boyfriend|girlfriend|haram)\\b","\\bzina\\b","\\bfornication\\b","\\bsex before marriage\\b","\\bpre ?marital sex\\b","\\bhave (?:a )?crush\\b","\\bin love with\\b","\\bfalling in love\\b","\\bwant to get married but\\b","\\bhow to find (?:a )?(?:spouse|husband|wife)\\b","\\bfind (?:a )?(?:spouse|husband|wife|partner)\\b"],t.s)
B.aVj=s(["\\b(?:non muslim|christian|jewish|hindu)\\b","\\b(?:guilt\\w*|ashamed|regret\\w*|heartbroken|broke up|breakup|cheated|cry\\w*|depress\\w*|lonely|i feel)\\b"],t.s)
B.amL=new A.aN("dating-relationships",B.aH0,B.aVj,"I am not assuming anything about you, and this is only the general guidance. Islam does not allow a private romantic relationship (boyfriend or girlfriend) outside marriage, but it does give a clean path to love and companionship, which is marriage.\n\nWhat the texts say: Islam closes the doors that lead to wrongdoing, so private meetings, flirting and physical contact outside marriage are avoided. Men and women are told to lower their gaze and guard themselves (Qur'an 24:30). The Prophet \ufdfa also said: It is not permissible for a man to be alone with a woman (Sahih al-Bukhari 3006).\n\nWhat is allowed: meeting a possible spouse with a family member present, asking about each other's religion and character, speaking about marriage openly, and then proceeding quickly to engagement and nikah. Many Muslim couples meet through family, community, mosque events or trusted marriage services. Love in marriage is a sign of Allah: \"He placed between you affection and mercy\" (Qur'an 30:21).\n\nIf you have already crossed the line, or have a relationship now, do not despair. Stop what is forbidden, turn to Allah in repentance, and if you wish to be together, ask for her or his hand through the family. Allah says: \"do not despair of the mercy of Allah\" (Qur'an 39:53). If you want, I can say more about repentance or about finding a spouse.\n\nSource: Qur'an 24:30, 30:21, 39:53; Sahih al-Bukhari 3006.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-nikah]\n[GO:/academy/family-life]","dating",!0,"")
B.b5Y=s(["\\bpolygam\\w*\\b","\\bpolygyn\\w*\\b","\\b(?:four|4|two|second|more than one) wi(?:fe|ves)\\b","\\bmarry (?:two|three|four|more than one)\\b","\\bwhy can (?:a )?(?:muslim )?(?:man|men) (?:have|marry) (?:more|four|multiple)\\b","\\bsecond marriage\\b","\\bsecond wife\\b"],t.s)
B.alg=new A.aN("polygamy",B.b5Y,B.b,"Islam permits a man to marry up to four wives, with a strict condition of justice. The Qur'an says: \"marry those that please you of [other] women, two or three or four. But if you fear that you will not be just, then [marry only] one\" (Qur'an 4:3). And it also says that perfect equality in feeling is beyond people (Qur'an 4:129), so what is required is fairness in what he can control: housing, maintenance, time and treatment. Not being able to be just means he should marry only one.\n\nIt is a permission, not a duty or a recommendation for everyone, and many Muslims never practise it. Cruelty or neglect of a wife is a sin, and a woman can set a condition in her marriage contract that her husband will not take a second wife, which many scholars accept. The law of the country you live in also applies, and in many countries a second marriage needs to be registered or is not allowed.\n\nScholars differ on whether the first wife's consent is needed; a husband should, at minimum, be honest with her and fair.\n\nSource: Qur'an 4:3, 4:129.","polygamy",!0,"")
B.aZg=s(["\\b(?:rights|duties|responsibilit\\w*|obligations?) of (?:a |the )?(?:husband|wife|spouse|husbands|wives)\\b","\\b(?:husband|wife) (?:and|or) (?:wife|husband)\\b.*\\b(?:rights|duties)\\b","\\bwhat (?:does|do) (?:a )?(?:husband|wife) (?:owe|have to)\\b","\\bhow (?:to|can i|do i) be (?:a )?(?:good|better) (?:husband|wife|spouse)\\b","\\bgood (?:husband|wife)\\b","\\bmarriage (?:advice|problems?|tips)\\b","\\b(?:fight|fighting|argue|arguing|quarrel\\w*) with (?:my )?(?:husband|wife|spouse)\\b","\\bmy (?:husband|wife) (?:and i|doesnt|does not|wont|will not|is not|ignores)\\b","\\bmaintenance\\b.*\\b(?:wife|husband)\\b"],t.s)
B.b17=s(["\\b(?:hits?|beats?|abus\\w*|afraid|scared)\\b"],t.s)
B.amY=new A.aN("spouse-rights",B.aZg,B.b17,"Marriage in Islam is a partnership with rights on both sides; the duties are not on one side only. Allah says \"And due to the wives is similar to what is expected of them, according to what is reasonable\" (Qur'an 2:228), and calls the couple a source of \"affection and mercy\" (Qur'an 30:21).\n\nFor the husband: to provide for his family, to treat his wife well and live with her in kindness (Qur'an 4:19). Abu Hurairah narrated the Prophet said the most complete of the believers in faith is the one with the best character, and the best of you are those who are best to your women (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 1162, hasan sahih per al-Albani). And: The best of you is the one who is best to his wife, and I am the best of you to my wives (Sunan Ibn Majah 1977, hasan per Darussalam).\n\nFor the wife: to keep the trust of the marriage, to protect the home and herself in his absence, and, as the scholars explain, to cooperate with her husband in what is right (Qur'an 4:34 describes righteous women as \"devoutly obedient, guarding in [the husband's] absence what Allah would have them guard\" (Qur'an 4:34)). Both should be honest and loyal to each other.\n\nIf you disagree, speak calmly, forgive the small things (Qur'an 4:19 says perhaps you dislike a thing and Allah makes therein much good), and ask family or a counsellor to help you, as in Qur'an 4:35. Abu Huraira reported the Prophet said act kindly towards women, and a believing man should not hate a believing woman: if he dislikes one of her characteristics, he will be pleased with another (Sahih Muslim 1468).\n\nSource: Qur'an 2:228, 30:21, 4:19, 4:34, 4:35; Jami' at-Tirmidhi 1162, hasan sahih per al-Albani; Sunan Ibn Majah 1977, hasan per Darussalam; Sahih Muslim 1468.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-nikah]","marriage",!0,"")
B.b18=s(["\\bparents?\\b","\\bmother\\b","\\bmom\\b","\\bmum\\b","\\bfather\\b","\\bdad\\b","\\bobey (?:my )?(?:parents|mother|father)\\b","\\bdisobey\\w*\\b.*\\b(?:parents|mother|father)\\b","\\brespect(?:ing)? (?:my )?(?:parents|mother|father|elders)\\b","\\bnursing home\\b","\\bold age home\\b","\\bcare for (?:my )?(?:old|elderly|aging)\\b","\\bparents? (?:are|arent|is) (?:non muslim|not muslim|divorced|angry|upset|unhappy)\\b","\\bparents? (?:dont|do not|wont|will not) (?:let|allow|accept|support|approve)\\b","\\bparents? (?:want|force|forcing)\\b","\\bbirr\\b","\\bwalidain\\b","\\bparent\\w* (?:rights?|status|rank)\\b","\\bparadise.*\\bmothers?\\b","\\bmothers? s? ?feet\\b"],t.s)
B.b6s=s(["\\b(?:prophets (?:mother|father|parents)|aminah|abdullah)\\b","\\b(?:game|story|stories)\\b","\\bin law\\b","\\b(?:fasting|zakat)\\b","\\b(?:dua|duas|supplication|supplications|du a)\\b","\\b(?:died|passed away|funeral|janazah)\\b"],t.s)
B.akV=new A.aN("parents",B.b18,B.b6s,"Honouring parents is one of the greatest duties in Islam, second only to worship of Allah in the Qur'an: \"your Lord has decreed that you not worship except Him, and to parents, good treatment. Whether one or both of them reach old age [while] with you, say not to them [so much as], \"uff,\" and do not repel them but speak to them a noble word\" (Qur'an 17:23). Qur'an 31:14 reminds that the mother carried the child in weakness upon weakness.\n\nMother first, then father. A man asked the Prophet who is more entitled to the best companionship, and he said your mother three times and then your father (Sahih al-Bukhari 5971). Staying with a mother who needs you can come before even going out to fight. Jahimah asked to go out and fight and the Prophet asked if he had a mother, and told him to stay with her, for Paradise is beneath her feet (Sunan an-Nasa'i 3104, hasan sahih per al-Albani). The Prophet \ufdfa also said that a person who finds his parents in old age and does not enter Paradise because of them is humbled (Sahih Muslim 2551).\n\nLimits: obedience is for what is good and lawful. If they order a sin, you do not obey, but you stay kind: \"do not obey them but accompany them in [this] world with appropriate kindness\" (Qur'an 31:15). That holds even for parents who are not Muslim. The Prophet said no obedience for evil deeds, obedience is required only in what is good (Sahih al-Bukhari 7257).\n\nIn practice: visit, call, serve them, make du'a for them, and care for them in old age at home where you can; if you truly cannot, a good nursing arrangement with frequent visits is a last resort, not a first choice. If a parent is hard on you, patience and gentle words remain your duty, while you protect yourself from real harm. Ask me about a specific situation if you wish.\n\nSource: Qur'an 17:23, 31:14, 31:15; Sahih al-Bukhari 5971, 7257; Sunan an-Nasa'i 3104, hasan sahih per al-Albani; Sahih Muslim 2551.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-parents]","parents",!0,"")
B.b2o=s(["\\b(?:raise|raising|rais\\w*|teach\\w*|bring up|bringing up) (?:my |our |the )?(?:kids?|children|child|son|daughter|boy|girl|teen\\w*)\\b","\\bparenting\\b","\\b(?:kids?|children|child|son|daughter)\\b.*\\b(?:pray|prayer|salah|quran|fast|hijab|islam)\\b","\\bwhen (?:should|do|does) (?:a )?(?:kids?|child|children|boy|girl)\\b.*\\b(?:pray|start|fast|hijab|wear)\\b","\\bchildrens rights\\b","\\brights of (?:the )?(?:child|children|kids)\\b","\\bdiscipline (?:my )?(?:kids?|children|child)\\b","\\bhit(?:ting)? (?:my |a )?(?:kids?|children|child)\\b","\\bbeat(?:ing)? (?:my |a )?(?:kids?|children|child)\\b","\\bspank\\w* (?:my |a )?(?:kids?|children|child)\\b","\\bat what age\\b.*\\b(?:pray|fast|hijab)\\b"],t.s)
B.aHP=s(["\\b(?:app|screen|kids mode|kids section|kids zone|child mode|game|games)\\b"],t.s)
B.am2=new A.aN("children-parenting",B.b2o,B.aHP,"Children are a trust. Allah says: \"O you who have believed, protect yourselves and your families from a Fire\" (Qur'an 66:6), and the Prophet \ufdfa showed mercy to children, saying Whoever is not merciful to others will not be treated mercifully (Sahih al-Bukhari 5997).\n\nWorship: parents are told to teach it gradually. The Prophet said command a boy to pray when he reaches the age of seven years, and when he becomes ten, beat him for prayer (Sunan Abu Dawud 494, hasan sahih per al-Albani). Scholars explain the beating as only a light, non-injurious reminder, if at all, and most advise gentleness, patience and example instead. Fasting and hijab are not obligatory until puberty; families begin training earlier in a gentle way, with encouragement and no pressure that harms the child.\n\nFairness: do not favour one child over another in gifts. The Prophet \ufdfa asked a man whether he had given the like of the gift to each of his sons (Sahih al-Bukhari 2587). Never kill or harm children from fear of poverty: \"do not kill your children for fear of poverty. We provide for them and for you\" (Qur'an 17:31).\n\nHitting a child as a punishment in anger, or in a way that hurts or leaves a mark, is not from the guidance of the Prophet \ufdfa. Teach by love, example, du'a and patience. The For Children section of this app is made for children to learn in a safe way.\n\nSource: Qur'an 66:6, 17:31; Sahih al-Bukhari 5997, 2587; Sunan Abu Dawud 494, hasan sahih per al-Albani.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-children-deen]","children",!0,"")
B.b8i=s(["\\bpillars? of (?:iman|imaan|faith|belief)\\b","\\b(?:six|6) (?:pillars|articles) of (?:iman|imaan|faith|belief)\\b","\\barticles of (?:faith|belief|iman)\\b","\\bwhat (?:do|does) (?:muslims?|islam) believe\\b","\\bwhat is iman\\b","\\bwhat is (?:imaan|faith)$","\\bbasic beliefs? (?:of|in) islam\\b","\\bbeliefs? of (?:muslims?|islam)\\b","\\bwhat must (?:a )?muslim believe\\b"],t.s)
B.anh=new A.aN("pillars-iman",B.b8i,B.b,"Iman (faith) has six articles. The angel Jibreel once asked the Prophet \ufdfa about iman. The Prophet said that iman is to affirm your faith in Allah, in His angels, in His Books, in His Messengers, in the Day of Judgment, and to affirm your faith in the divine decree about good and evil (Sahih Muslim 8).\n\n1. Allah: that He is One, with no partner (Qur'an 112:1).\n2. His angels.\n3. His books: the Qur'an, and the earlier revelations as they were sent down (such as the Torah and the Gospel).\n4. His messengers: from Adam to Muhammad \ufdfa, the last of them (Qur'an 33:40).\n5. The Last Day: the resurrection, reckoning, Paradise and the Fire.\n6. The divine decree (qadar): that everything happens by Allah's knowledge and will, while we are still responsible for our choices.\n\nThe Qur'an lists the first five in Qur'an 2:285 and Qur'an 4:136. The five pillars of Islam are separate: the shahada, prayer, zakat, fasting in Ramadan and Hajj (Sahih al-Bukhari 8).\n\nSource: Sahih Muslim 8; Qur'an 112:1, 33:40, 2:285, 4:136; Sahih al-Bukhari 8.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-what-is-islam]\n[GO:/new-muslim]","iman",!0,"")
B.aIc=s(["\\b(?:become|becoming|convert|converting|revert|reverting|embrace|embracing|accept|accepting|join) (?:to )?(?:a )?(?:islam|muslim)\\b","\\bi want to (?:become|be|convert|revert|embrace|accept|join)\\b.*\\b(?:muslim|islam)\\b","\\bhow (?:do|can|to) (?:i|someone|one|you) (?:become|convert|revert|embrace|accept|join)\\b","\\bshahada\\b","\\bshahadah\\b","\\btestimony of faith\\b","\\bconversion to islam\\b","\\bhow to (?:become|convert|revert) \\w*\\b","\\bnew muslim\\b","\\bjust (?:converted|reverted|became a muslim)\\b","\\bi (?:recently )?(?:converted|reverted)\\b","\\bwhat do i need to do to (?:become|be) (?:a )?muslim\\b","\\bwelcome to islam\\b"],t.s)
B.al3=new A.aN("become-muslim",B.aIc,B.b,'Welcome. Becoming a Muslim is simple: say the shahada (the declaration of faith), knowing what it means and meaning it:\n\n\u0623\u0634\u0647\u062f \u0623\u0646 \u0644\u0627 \u0625\u0644\u0647 \u0625\u0644\u0627 \u0627\u0644\u0644\u0647 \u0648\u0623\u0634\u0647\u062f \u0623\u0646 \u0645\u062d\u0645\u062f\u0627\u064b \u0631\u0633\u0648\u0644 \u0627\u0644\u0644\u0647\nAsh-hadu an la ilaha illa Allah, wa ash-hadu anna Muhammadan rasulu Allah.\n"I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah."\n\nYou can say it alone, to Allah, at any time; no priest, certificate or special place is needed. Many people say it before a witness or at a mosque so that the community knows and can help, and that is a good thing to do, but it is not what makes you a Muslim. This is the first of the five pillars (Sahih al-Bukhari 8).\n\nGood to know:\n- Your earlier sins are forgiven. Amr ibn al-As reported the Prophet asked him, Are you not aware that Islam wipes out all the previous misdeeds? (Sahih Muslim 121).\n- Taking a bath on accepting Islam: Qays ibn Asim came to the Prophet intending to embrace Islam, and the Prophet commanded him to take a bath with water boiled with the leaves of the lote tree (Sunan Abu Dawud 355, sahih per Darussalam). Scholars differ on whether this bath is required or only recommended. All agree it is good, so if you can, take it.\n- Learn step by step: first to pray (see the How to Pray guide), then fasting, and the rest as you can. Nobody expects you to know everything at once. Allah says "has not placed upon you in the religion any difficulty" (Qur\'an 22:78).\n\nThe New Muslim path in this app walks you through the basics. If you want to talk with someone, a nearby masjid will welcome you.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 8; Sahih Muslim 121; Sunan Abu Dawud 355, sahih per Darussalam; Qur\'an 22:78.\n[GO:/new-muslim]\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-prayer]',"shahada",!0,"")
B.bgk=s(["\\bbismillah\\b","\\bbismillahi\\b","\\bbasmala\\w*\\b","\\bbasmalah\\b","\\bin the name of allah\\b.*\\bmean\\w*\\b","\\bwhy (?:do )?muslims? say bismillah\\b","\\bwhen (?:to|should i) say bismillah\\b"],t.s)
B.bkg=s(["\\b(?:slaughter\\w*|halal meat|zabiha)\\b"],t.s)
B.ams=new A.aN("bismillah-meaning",B.bgk,B.bkg,'Bismillah is short for "Bismillahi r-Rahmani r-Rahim": "In the name of Allah, the Entirely Merciful, the Especially Merciful" (Qur\'an 1:1). It is the first verse of the Qur\'an\'s opening chapter, and it begins every surah except Surah At-Tawbah.\n\nMuslims say it to begin an action in Allah\'s name: eating, drinking, entering the home, starting wudu, studying, travelling. Teach children to begin with Allah\'s name. Umar ibn Abi Salama said the Prophet told him to mention the name of Allah, eat with his right hand and eat from what is nearest to him (Sahih al-Bukhari 5376). Saying "Bismillah" reminds you that you do it with Allah\'s help and for what He allows.\n\nAt the end, "Alhamdulillah" (all praise is due to Allah) is said to thank Him.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 1:1; Sahih al-Bukhari 5376.\n[GO:/adhkar]',"bismillah",!0,"")
B.b0N=s(["\\bassalamu? ?alaikum\\b","\\bas salamu? ?alaykum\\b","\\bassalamualaikum\\b","\\bsalam alaikum\\b","\\bsalaam\\b","\\bwhat (?:does|is) salam\\b","\\bmeaning of (?:salam|salaam|assalam\\w*)\\b","\\bhow (?:do|to) (?:i )?(?:greet|reply to) (?:a )?(?:muslim|salam)\\b","\\bwa alaikum\\b","\\bwalaikum\\b","\\bislamic greeting\\b","\\bmuslim greeting\\b","^greet\\w*$"],t.s)
B.amy=new A.aN("salam-meaning",B.b0N,B.A0,'"As-salamu alaykum" means "peace be upon you". The reply is "Wa alaykumu s-salam" ("and upon you peace"), and Muslims often add "wa rahmatullahi wa barakatuh" ("and Allah\'s mercy and blessings"). It is the greeting of Muslims, and it is also a prayer for the other person.\n\nThe Qur\'an commands: "And when you are greeted with a greeting, greet [in return] with one better than it or [at least] return it [in a like manner]" (Qur\'an 4:86). The Prophet \ufdfa was asked what is best in Islam and said to feed people and to greet those you know and those you do not know (Sahih al-Bukhari 12).\n\nSource: Qur\'an 4:86; Sahih al-Bukhari 12.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-greeting]',"salam",!0,"")
B.bjW=s(["\\bisra\\b","\\bmiraj\\b","\\bmiraj\\b","\\bnight journey\\b","\\bascension\\b","\\bburaq\\b","\\bsidrat\\b","\\blote tree\\b","\\bwhy (?:do )?(?:we|muslims?) pray (?:five|5) times\\b","\\bwhere (?:did|do) the (?:five|5) prayers come from\\b","\\bwhen (?:was|did) the (?:five|5) prayers? (?:made|become) (?:obligatory|fard)\\b"],t.s)
B.amt=new A.aN("isra-miraj",B.bjW,B.b,"The Isra is the night journey in which Allah took the Prophet \ufdfa from the Sacred Mosque in Makkah to the Aqsa Mosque in Jerusalem: \"Exalted is He who took His Servant by night from al-Masjid al-Haram to al-Masjid al-Aqsa, whose surroundings We have blessed, to show him of Our signs\" (Qur'an 17:1). The Mi'raj is his ascension through the heavens to the Lote Tree of the Utmost Boundary, mentioned in Qur'an 53:13-18.\n\nThe hadith adds details: he was carried on the Buraq, led the prophets in prayer in Jerusalem (Sahih Muslim 162), and he was ascended through the heavens. In the report in Bukhari, Allah first enjoined fifty daily prayers; on Musa's advice the Prophet \ufdfa asked for a reduction until it became five, and Allah said these five are equal to fifty in reward (Sahih al-Bukhari 349).\n\nThis is why the five daily prayers are called a gift from the Mi'raj. Scholars differ on the exact date of the night, and the Prophet \ufdfa did not set a day for celebrating it, so there is no authentic date to mark.\n\nSource: Qur'an 17:1, 53:13; Sahih Muslim 162; Sahih al-Bukhari 349.","isra",!0,"")
B.b01=s(["\\b(?:four|4) (?:rightly guided )?caliphs?\\b","\\brightly guided caliphs?\\b","\\brashidun\\b","\\bkhulafa\\b","\\bkhulafa e rashideen\\b","\\bkhulafa ar rashidun\\b","\\bwho (?:was|were) the (?:first|second|third|fourth) caliphs?\\b","\\bwho (?:was|is) the first caliph\\b","\\bsucceeded the prophet\\b","\\bafter the prophet died\\b","\\bsuccessors? of the prophet\\b"],t.s)
B.amV=new A.aN("four-caliphs",B.b01,B.b,"The four Rightly Guided Caliphs (al-Khulafa ar-Rashidun) led the Muslim community after the Prophet \ufdfa passed away in the year 11 AH (632 CE), in this order:\n1. Abu Bakr as-Siddiq (11-13 AH), the Prophet's close companion in the cave of the Hijrah (Qur'an 9:40).\n2. Umar ibn al-Khattab (13-23 AH), who expanded the state and gathered the people behind one imam for Taraweeh.\n3. Uthman ibn Affan (23-35 AH), under whom the Qur'an was copied into the standard written copies.\n4. Ali ibn Abi Talib (35-40 AH), cousin and son-in-law of the Prophet \ufdfa.\n\nAll four were among the ten the Prophet \ufdfa gave glad tidings of Paradise (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 3747, sahih per al-Albani). Sunni Muslims count these four as the Rightly Guided Caliphs; Shia Muslims hold a different view on the question of leadership after the Prophet \ufdfa (see the answer on Sunni and Shia). This app does not rank Muslim groups.\n\nSource: Qur'an 9:40; Jami' at-Tirmidhi 3747, sahih per al-Albani.\n[GO:/stories]","caliphs",!0,"")
B.bnj=s(["\\bcompanions? of the prophet\\b","\\bthe prophets companions\\b","\\bsahabah?\\b","\\bsahabi\\b","\\bprophets companions\\b","\\bwho (?:are|were) the (?:companions|sahabah)\\b","^(?:tell me about )?the (?:prophets )?companions\\b","\\bthe ten promised paradise\\b","\\basharah mubashshar\\w*\\b","\\bten companions\\b"],t.s)
B.akR=new A.aN("companions-sahabah",B.bnj,B.b,"A companion (sahabi) is a person who met the Prophet \ufdfa, believed in him, and died as a Muslim. They learned Islam from him directly and passed it to us, and they are the best generation. Allah says: \"And the first forerunners [in the faith] among the Muhajireen and the Ansar and those who followed them with good conduct - Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him\" (Qur'an 9:100). Abdullah reported the Prophet said the best people are those living in my generation, and then those who will follow them, and then those who will follow the latter (Sahih al-Bukhari 3651).\n\nAmong them: the four Rightly Guided Caliphs (Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman, Ali); the ten given glad tidings of Paradise (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 3747, sahih per al-Albani); the Prophet's wives and daughters; and many Muhajirun and Ansar. Their stories are in the Stories section of this app.\n\nSource: Qur'an 9:100; Sahih al-Bukhari 3651; Jami' at-Tirmidhi 3747, sahih per al-Albani.\n[GO:/stories]","companions",!0,"")
B.aUk=s(["\\bbadr\\b","\\bbattle of badr\\b","\\bghazwat? badr\\b","\\bday of the criterion\\b","\\bfurqan\\b.*\\bbattle\\b"],t.s)
B.al7=new A.aN("badr",B.aUk,B.b,"Badr was the first major battle between the Muslims of Madinah and the Quraysh of Makkah, fought in Ramadan in the year 2 AH. The Muslims were few and poorly armed, the Makkan army about three times as many, and Allah gave the believers victory: \"And already had Allah given you victory at [the battle of] Badr while you were few in number\" (Qur'an 3:123).\n\nOn the numbers, Umar reported that on the day of Badr the Prophet cast a glance at the polytheists, who were one thousand, while his own companions were three hundred and nineteen, then turned towards the qiblah and made du'a to his Lord (Sahih Muslim 1763). Al-Bara said that the Badr fighters were about three hundred and ten and some, like the companions of Talut (Sahih al-Bukhari 3959). Allah reminded them that He sent help with the angels (Qur'an 8:9) and that victory is only from Him (Qur'an 3:126).\n\nThe Qur'an calls it the day of the Furqan (the Criterion), when truth was separated from falsehood. Those who fought at Badr are honoured among the companions.\n\nSource: Qur'an 3:123, 8:9, 3:126; Sahih Muslim 1763; Sahih al-Bukhari 3959.","badr",!0,"")
B.aKN=s(["\\bkarbala\\b","\\bkerbela\\b","\\bashura\\b.*\\b(?:husain|husayn|hussain|karbala)\\b","\\bhusayn\\b","\\bhusain\\b","\\bhussain\\b","\\bimam hussain\\b","\\bday of ashura\\b"],t.s)
B.aSv=s(["\\b(?:fast|fasting|musa|moses|pharaoh)\\b"],t.s)
B.alZ=new A.aN("karbala",B.aKN,B.aSv,"Karbala is a place in Iraq where Husayn ibn Ali, the grandson of the Prophet \ufdfa, was killed on 10 Muharram in the year 61 AH (680 CE), together with members of his family and companions, by the forces of the Umayyad caliph Yazid. Husayn and his brother Hasan are loved by Muslims of every school: Abu Sa'id narrated the Messenger of Allah said Al-Hasan and Al-Husayn are the chiefs of the youths of Paradise (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 3768, sahih per Darussalam).\n\nMuslims of all backgrounds regard the killing of the Prophet's grandson as a great tragedy and a grave wrong. They differ in how it is remembered and in what it means politically; Shia communities mourn it publicly in Muharram, while Sunni communities generally remember it without formal mourning rites. This app does not take sides in those differences; for details, ask a scholar of the tradition you follow.\n\nNote that 10 Muharram, Ashura, is also a day of fasting for all Muslims, for reasons that go back to the days of Musa; ask me about fasting on Ashura.\n\nSource: Jami' at-Tirmidhi 3768, sahih per Darussalam.","karbala",!0,"")
B.bhm=s(["\\bsunni\\b","\\bsunnis\\b","\\bshia\\b","\\bshia\\b","\\bshiite\\b","\\bshiite\\b","\\bsunni (?:and|vs|versus|or) shia\\b","\\bshia (?:and|vs|versus|or) sunni\\b","\\bdifference between sunni\\b","\\bwhich (?:sect|group) (?:is|are) (?:right|correct|true|better)\\b","\\bsects? (?:in|of) islam\\b","\\bwhy (?:are|do) (?:there|muslims) (?:so many )?(?:sects|divisions|groups)\\b","\\bmuslim sects\\b","\\bwhich is better\\b.*\\b(?:sunni|shia|school|madhhab)\\b"],t.s)
B.b4h=s(["\\b(?:change|choose|school of law|my school|madhab|madhhab setting)\\b.*\\b(?:app|setting|settings|menu)\\b"],t.s)
B.amM=new A.aN("sunni-shia",B.bhm,B.b4h,"Sunni and Shia Muslims share the core of Islam: they believe in one God, in Muhammad \ufdfa as His final messenger (Qur'an 33:40), in the Qur'an, the angels, the Last Day, and they pray towards the same Ka'bah, fast in Ramadan, give zakat, and perform Hajj.\n\nThe main historical difference is over who was entitled to lead the community after the Prophet \ufdfa: Sunnis accept the leadership of Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman and Ali in that order, while Shia hold that Ali and his descendants were the rightful leaders. From this follow differences in some hadith collections, in schools of law, and in some practices.\n\nI cannot say which group is \"better\", and this app does not rank Muslims. Allah tells believers to hold together and not divide: \"And hold firmly to the rope of Allah all together and do not become divided\" (Qur'an 3:103), and \"The believers are but brothers, so make settlement between your brothers\" (Qur'an 49:10). For questions of belief and practice, ask a scholar of the tradition you follow.\n\nSource: Qur'an 33:40, 3:103, 49:10.\n[GO:/sources/policy]","sects",!0,"")
B.aVW=s(["\\bsurah? (?:al )?(?:asr|al asr|103)\\b","\\bchapter (?:al )?asr\\b","\\bsurat(?:ul)? ?(?:al )?asr\\b","\\bsurah by time\\b","\\bwal asr\\b","\\bwal-asr\\b","\\bmankind is in loss\\b","\\bquran 103\\b","\\b103[: ]1\\b"],t.s)
B.am1=new A.aN("surah-asr",B.aVW,B.b,"Surah Al-Asr (103) is the shortest surah that sums up the way out of loss:\n\n\"By time, Indeed, mankind is in loss, Except for those who have believed and done righteous deeds and advised each other to truth and advised each other to patience.\" (Qur'an 103:1-3)\n\nIt says that every person is at a loss unless four things come together: faith, righteous deeds, advising one another to the truth, and advising one another to patience. Time is the capital of life, and it passes either in gain or in loss. You can read and hear the surah in the Qur'an screen.\n\nSource: Qur'an 103:1-3.\n[GO:/quran]","asr",!0,"")
B.aNh=s(["\\bibrahim\\b.*\\b(?:sacrific\\w*|son|ismail|ishmael|isaac|dream|knife)\\b","\\babraham\\b.*\\b(?:sacrific\\w*|son|ishmael|isaac|dream|knife)\\b","\\bstory of (?:ibrahim|abraham)\\b","\\bwhy (?:do )?muslims? sacrifice\\b","\\b(?:sacrifice|qurbani|udhiyah)\\b.*\\b(?:ibrahim|abraham|eid)\\b","\\bwhat is (?:the )?(?:eid al adha|eid ul adha|bakra eid)\\b","\\bwhy (?:do )?(?:we|muslims?) celebrate eid al adha\\b"],t.s)
B.an3=new A.aN("ibrahim-sacrifice",B.aNh,B.b,'The Qur\'an tells that Ibrahim (Abraham) saw in a dream that he must sacrifice his son, and the son agreed: "indeed I have seen in a dream that I [must] sacrifice you, so see what you think" (Qur\'an 37:102). The son answered: "do as you are commanded. You will find me, if Allah wills, of the steadfast" (Qur\'an 37:102). When both had submitted, Allah called to him: "You have fulfilled the vision" (Qur\'an 37:105), and "We ransomed him with a great sacrifice" (Qur\'an 37:107), a ram in place of the son.\n\nIt was a trial of obedience and love, and shows that Allah does not want human life but obedience and sacrifice from the heart. The Qur\'an does not name the son in these verses; the majority of scholars say it was Ismail, and some say Ishaq.\n\nEid al-Adha remembers this. Muslims who can afford it offer a sacrifice of an animal (udhiyah/qurbani) and share the meat with family and the poor, and pilgrims complete the rites of Hajj. The sacrifice is itself an act of worship, not a payment for sins.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 37:102, 37:105, 37:107.',"ibrahim",!0,"")
B.aHN=s(["\\bwho (?:was|is) (?:prophet )?(?:muhammad|mohammed|mohammad)\\b","^(?:tell me about )?(?:prophet |the prophet )?(?:muhammad|mohammed|mohammad)\\b","\\bbiography of (?:the )?(?:prophet|muhammad)\\b","\\blife of (?:the )?prophet\\b","\\bseerah\\b.*\\b(?:summary|overview|short|brief)\\b","\\bbrief (?:life|history) of (?:the )?prophet\\b","\\bwhen was (?:the )?prophet (?:muhammad )?(?:born|died)\\b","\\bhow old was (?:the )?prophet\\b","\\bwhere was (?:the )?prophet (?:born|buried)\\b","\\bwho is the prophet\\b","\\bwho was the prophet\\b","\\bwho (?:was|is) the last prophet\\b","\\bwho is the last prophet\\b","\\bwhat did the prophet (?:do|look like|eat)\\b"],t.s)
B.b1y=s(["\\b(?:wives|wife|marriage|aisha|ayesha|khadijah|khadija|sahabah|companions)\\b","\\b(?:picture|image|draw|photo)\\b","\\b(?:saqib|iqbal|shaykh|sheikh|shaikh|ustadh|mufti|imam|credentials|qualifications?)\\b"],t.s)
B.an_=new A.aN("prophet-biography",B.aHN,B.b1y,'Muhammad \ufdfa is the last Messenger of Allah ("Muhammad is not the father of [any] one of your men, but [he is] the Messenger of Allah and last of the prophets" (Qur\'an 33:40)). In outline:\n- He was born in Makkah, around 570 CE, in the tribe of Quraysh; he was orphaned young and was known among his people as the truthful and trustworthy (as-Sadiq al-Amin).\n- At the age of forty he began to receive the revelation. Ibn Abbas said the Prophet started receiving the Divine Inspiration at the age of forty, stayed in Makkah for thirteen years, was ordered to migrate, lived as an emigrant for ten years and died at sixty-three (Sahih al-Bukhari 3902). It began in the cave of Hira, with the first words of Surah Al-Alaq (Qur\'an 96:1): "Recite in the name of your Lord who created". Bukhari describes the beginning in Sahih al-Bukhari 3.\n- In Makkah he invited people to worship Allah alone, and many suffered for it. After the hardship at Ta\'if, when the people there did not respond, the angel of the mountains offered to crush them and he refused, hoping that Allah would bring from them people who worship Him alone (Sahih al-Bukhari 3231).\n- He migrated to Madinah (the Hijrah), built the first community there, and returned to Makkah in the conquest, a few years before his death.\n- He died in Madinah at the age of sixty-three (Sahih Muslim 2348). He left no wealth: Aisha said the Prophet died while his iron armor was mortgaged to a Jew for thirty sa of barley (Sahih al-Bukhari 2916).\n\nAllah describes him as "of a great moral character" (Qur\'an 68:4) and as "a mercy to the worlds" (Qur\'an 21:107). The Seerah section of the Academy has his life in detail. His image is never drawn in Islam, out of respect.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 33:40, 96:1, 68:4, 21:107; Sahih al-Bukhari 3902, 3, 3231, 2916; Sahih Muslim 2348.\n[GO:/academy/seerah]',"prophet",!0,"")
B.aTd=s(["\\bwhat is (?:the )?(?:quran|quran|koran)\\b","\\bwho wrote (?:the )?(?:quran|quran|koran)\\b","\\bwhat is the holy book\\b","\\bwhat is islams holy book\\b","\\bis the (?:quran|quran|koran) (?:changed|preserved|authentic|real|original|corrupted)\\b","\\bhow many (?:surahs|chapters|suras)\\b","\\bhow (?:was|is) the (?:quran|quran|koran) (?:revealed|compiled|preserved|collected)\\b","^(?:tell me about )?the (?:quran|quran|koran)\\b","\\bwhen was the (?:quran|quran|koran) revealed\\b","\\bquran (?:overview|introduction|summary)\\b","\\bwhy (?:is|was) the (?:quran|quran) in arabic\\b"],t.s)
B.bbq=s(["\\b(?:in this app|screen|bookmark|download|tafsir tab|reciter|audio)\\b","\\bvs\\b","\\bdifference between\\b","\\b(?:game|games|quiz|trivia|play)\\b"],t.s)
B.akS=new A.aN("quran-what",B.aTd,B.bbq,"The Qur'an is the word of Allah, revealed to the Prophet Muhammad \ufdfa through the angel Jibreel (Qur'an 2:97), in Arabic, in stages over about twenty-three years (the thirteen years in Makkah and ten in Madinah of Sahih al-Bukhari 3902). It began in the month of Ramadan: \"The month of Ramadhan [is that] in which was revealed the Qur'an, a guidance for the people\" (Qur'an 2:185), on the Night of Decree (Qur'an 97:1).\n\nIt has 114 chapters (surahs), from Al-Fatihah to An-Nas. It is a guidance for belief, worship, character and daily life, and it is the central miracle of Islam. Allah promised to guard it: \"Indeed, it is We who sent down the Qur'an and indeed, We will be its guardian\" (Qur'an 15:9). It was memorised and written down during the Prophet's life, and Muslims have recited it by heart ever since, which is why the same text is read across the world.\n\nReading it, understanding it, and acting on it are all rewarded. You can read and hear it in the Qur'an screen of this app, and the Hifz planner helps you memorise it.\n\nSource: Qur'an 2:97, 2:185, 97:1, 15:9; Sahih al-Bukhari 3902.\n[GO:/quran]","quran",!0,"")
B.b6a=s(["\\bwhat is (?:a |the )?hadith\\b","\\bwhat are hadiths?\\b","\\bwhat are ahadith\\b","\\bwhat is (?:the )?sunnah\\b","\\bwhat is sunnah\\b","\\bhadith (?:grades?|grading|classification|authenticity)\\b","\\bsahih (?:vs|versus|and|or) (?:hasan|daif|weak)\\b","\\bwhat is (?:a )?(?:sahih|hasan|daif|daif|weak|fabricated|mawdu) hadith\\b","\\bwhat does sahih mean\\b","\\bhow (?:do|can) (?:i|we) know (?:if )?(?:a )?hadith is (?:authentic|sahih|real)\\b","\\bsix books of hadith\\b","\\bsihah sitta\\b","\\bkutub al sittah\\b","\\bmajor hadith collections\\b","\\bhadith collections?\\b"],t.s)
